// Generated fact cards. Sources: world-countries 5.1.0 (ODbL), Wikidata (CC0), Wikipedia flag article summaries (CC BY-SA 4.0).
const COUNTRY_CARDS = {
  AFG: "Capital: Kabul. Currency: Afghan afghani (AFN). Languages listed as official or national: Dari, Pashto, Turkmen. People are called: Afghan. Area: 652,230 km2 (rank 40 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 33 N, 65 E. Population: about 41.5 million (2023). Highest point: Noshaq (7492 m). National anthem: This Is the Home of the Brave. Official languages and their families: Turkmen (language families: Eastern Oghuz, Oghuz, Common Turkic languages, Turkic); Uzbek (language families: Western Karluk, Karluk, Common Turkic languages, Turkic); Dari (language families: Persian, Southwestern Iranian, Western Iranian, Iranian, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European); Pamir (language families: Iranian, Eastern Iranian, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European); Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); Pashto (language families: Indo-Iranian, Eastern Iranian, Iranian, Indo-European); Pashayi (language families: Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European); Nuristani (language families: Indo-Iranian, Indo-European); Balochi (language families: Northwestern Iranian, Western Iranian, Iranian, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Form of government: unitary state, Emirate, islamic theocracy. Lowest point: Amu Darya. Internet domain: .af. Calling code: +93. Flag: The national flag of the Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan was adopted on 15 August 2021, with the Taliban's victory in the 2001–2021 war. It features a white field with a black Shahada, the Islamic declaration of belief, inscribed. Since the 20th century, Afghanistan has changed its national flag several times. During this period, the national flag had mostly black, red, and green colours.",
  AGO: "Capital: Luanda. Currency: Angolan kwanza (AOA). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese. People are called: Angolan. Area: 1,246,700 km2 (rank 22 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 12.5 S, 18.5 E. Population: about 36.7 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Moco (2619 m). National anthem: Angola Avante. Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .ao. Calling code: +244. Flag: The national flag of Angola is a horizontal bicolour of red and black, charged in the center with a yellow emblem consisting of a machete crossed by a half-cogwheel and crowned with a five-pointed star. It was adopted on 11 November 1975, when Angola became independent from Portugal following the thirteen-year Angolan War of Independence. The original meanings of the flag's colours and symbols accordingly referenced the war and colonial period, but they have since been reinterpreted to represent the Angolan people and society more broadly.",
  ALB: "Capital: Tirana. Currency: Albanian lek (ALL). Languages listed as official or national: Albanian. People are called: Albanian. Area: 28,748 km2 (rank 140 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 41 N, 20 E. Population: about 2.8 million (2023). Highest point: Korab (2764 m). National anthem: Himni i Flamurit. Official languages and their families: Albanian (language families: Indo-European). Form of government: parliamentary system. Lowest point: Adriatic Sea. Internet domain: .al. Calling code: +355. Flag: The national flag of Albania depicts a silhouetted black double-headed eagle in the center of a red background. The red stands for bravery, strength, valour and bloodshed, while the eagle – traditionally the symbol of Albanians – represents the sovereign state of Albania. The flag was established as the national flag of Albania when the country gained its independence from the Ottoman Empire in 1912.",
  AND: "Capital: Andorra la Vella. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Catalan. People are called: Andorran. Area: 468 km2 (rank 179 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 42.5 N, 1.5 E. Population: about 0.1 million (2026). Highest point: Coma Pedrosa (2942 m). National anthem: El gran Carlemany. Official languages and their families: Catalan (language families: Occitano-Romance, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: parliamentary coprincipality. Lowest point: Gran Valira. Internet domain: .ad. Calling code: +376. Flag: The national flag of Andorra features a vertical tricolour of blue, yellow, and red with the coat of arms of Andorra in the center. The centre yellow bar is slightly wider than the other two so that the ratio of bar widths is 8:9:8 with an overall flag ratio of 7:10.",
  ARE: "Capital: Abu Dhabi. Currency: United Arab Emirates dirham (AED). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Emirati. Area: 83,600 km2 (rank 114 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 24 N, 54 E. Population: about 10.1 million (2026). Highest point: Jebel Jais (1911 m). National anthem: Ishy Bilady. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, absolute monarchy, federal monarchy. Lowest point: Persian Gulf. Internet domain: .ae. Calling code: +971. Flag: The national flag of the United Arab Emirates contains the pan-Arab colors red, green, white, and black. It was designed in 1971 by Abdullah Mohammed Al Maainah, who was 19 years old at that time, and was adopted on 2 December 1971 after winning a nationwide flag design contest. The main theme of the flag's four colors is the sovereignty and unity of the Arab states.",
  ARG: "Capital: Buenos Aires. Currency: Argentine peso (ARS). Languages listed as official or national: Guaraní, Spanish. People are called: Argentine. Area: 2,780,400 km2 (rank 8 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 34 S, 64 W. Population: about 47.3 million (2022). National anthem: Argentine National Anthem. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Laguna del Carbón. Internet domain: .ar. Calling code: +54. Flag: The national flag of Argentina, often referred to as the Argentine flag, is a triband, composed of three equally wide horizontal bands coloured sky blue and pearly white. There are multiple interpretations on the reasons for those colors. The flag was created by Manuel Belgrano, in line with the creation of the Cockade of Argentina, and was first raised at the city of Rosario on 27 February 1812, during the Argentine War of Independence. The National Flag Memorial was later built on the site. The First Triumvirate did not approve the use of the flag, but the Assembly of the Year XIII allowed the use of the flag as a war flag. It was the Congress of Tucumán which finally designated it as the national flag in 1816. A yellow Sun of May was added to the center in 1818 as a war flag.",
  ARM: "Capital: Yerevan. Currency: Armenian dram (AMD). Languages listed as official or national: Armenian. People are called: Armenian. Area: 29,743 km2 (rank 138 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 40 N, 45 E. Highest point: Mount Aragats (4090 m). National anthem: Mer Hayrenik. Official languages and their families: Armenian (language families: Indo-European). Form of government: republic, parliamentary system, unitary state. Lowest point: Debed. Internet domain: .am. Calling code: +374. Flag: The national flag of Armenia, sometimes referred to as the Armenian tricolor, consists of three horizontal bands of equal width, red on the top, blue in the middle, and orange on the bottom. The Armenian Supreme Soviet (parliament) adopted the current flag on 24 August 1990, which was originally used during the First Republic of Armenia (1918–20).",
  ATG: "Capital: Saint John's. Currency: Eastern Caribbean dollar (XCD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Antiguan, Barbudan. Area: 442 km2 (rank 182 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 17.05 N, 61.8 W. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Obama (402 m). National anthem: God Save the King. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .ag. Calling code: +1268. Flag: The national flag of Antigua and Barbuda was adopted on 27 February 1967 to mark the achievement of self-government. A competition to design the flag was held in which more than 600 local people entered. The winning design was put forth by nationally well-known artist and sculptor Sir Reginald Samuel.",
  AUS: "Capital: Canberra. Currency: Australian dollar (AUD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Australian. Area: 7,692,024 km2 (rank 6 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 27 S, 133 E. Population: about 27.6 million (2025). Highest point: Mawson Peak (2745 m). National anthem: Advance Australia Fair. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, federation, representative democracy. Lowest point: Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre. Internet domain: .au. Calling code: +61. Flag: The national flag of Australia is based on the British Blue Ensign—a blue field with the Union Jack in the upper hoist quarter—featuring a large white seven-pointed star and a representation of the Southern Cross constellation, made up of five white stars.",
  AUT: "Capital: Vienna. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Austro-Bavarian German. People are called: Austrian. Area: 83,871 km2 (rank 113 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 47.33333333 N, 13.33333333 E. Population: about 9.0 million (2022). Highest point: Grossglockner (3798 m). National anthem: Land der Berge, Land am Strome. Official languages and their families: Austrian Sign Language; German (language families: West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: republic, semi-presidential system, federal parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Neusiedl Lake. Internet domain: .at. Calling code: +43. Flag: The national flag of Austria is a triband in the following order: red, white, and red.",
  AZE: "Capital: Baku. Currency: Azerbaijani manat (AZN). Languages listed as official or national: Azerbaijani, Russian. People are called: Azerbaijani. Area: 86,600 km2 (rank 112 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 40.5 N, 47.5 E. Population: about 10.2 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Bazardüzü (2706 m). National anthem: Azərbaycan marşı. Official languages and their families: Azerbaijani Sign Language; Azerbaijani (language families: Oghuz, Common Turkic languages, Turkic). Form of government: republic, semi-presidential system, unitary state. Lowest point: Caspian Sea. Internet domain: .az. Calling code: +994. Flag: The national flag of Azerbaijan, often referred to as the Tricolor flag, is a horizontal tricolor that features three equally sized bars of bright blue, red, and green; a white crescent; and a centred eight-pointed star. The flag has become the predominant and most recognizable symbol of Azerbaijan. The bright blue represents Azerbaijan's Turkic identity, the red represents progress, and the green represents Islam, which is Azerbaijan's majority religion.",
  BDI: "Capital: Gitega. Currency: Burundian franc (BIF). Languages listed as official or national: French, Kirundi. People are called: Burundian. Area: 27,834 km2 (rank 142 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 3.5 S, 30 E. Population: about 13.7 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Heha (2684 m). National anthem: Burundi Bwacu. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); Kirundi (language families: Great Lakes Bantu, Northeast Bantu, East Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo). Form of government: presidential system, representative democracy. Lowest point: Lake Tanganyika. Internet domain: .bi. Calling code: +257. Flag: The original national flag of Burundi was adopted after the Burundian independence from Belgium on 1 July 1962. It went through several revisions and now consists of a white saltire which divides the field into alternating red and green areas. The center of the saltire merges into a white disk, on which there are three red solid six-pointed stars outlined in green. The current ratio is 3:5, which was changed from 2:3 on 27 September 1982.",
  BEL: "Capital: Brussels. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: German, French, Dutch. People are called: Belgian. Area: 30,528 km2 (rank 136 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 50.83333333 N, 4 E. Population: about 11.8 million (2025). Highest point: Signal de Botrange (694 m). National anthem: The Brabançonne. Official languages and their families: German (language families: West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); Dutch (language families: Low Franconian, Franconian, West Germanic languages, High German, West Central German, Weser-Rhine Germanic, South Germanic, Old Germanic, North Sea Germanic, Central German, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: federation. Lowest point: De Moeren. Internet domain: .be. Calling code: +32. Flag: The national flag of the Kingdom of Belgium is a tricolour consisting of three equal vertical bands displaying the national colours: black, yellow, and red. The colours were taken from the coat of arms of the Duchy of Brabant, and the vertical design may be based on the flag of France. When flown, the black band is nearest the pole. It has the unusual proportions of 13∶15, and therefore, unlike the flags of Switzerland and the Vatican City, it is not a perfect square.",
  BEN: "Capital: Porto-Novo. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Beninese. Area: 112,622 km2 (rank 100 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 9.5 N, 2.25 E. Population: about 14.1 million (2023). Highest point: Mont Sokbaro (658 m). National anthem: L'Aube Nouvelle. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Bight of Benin. Internet domain: .bj. Calling code: +229. Flag: The national flag of Benin is a flag consisting of two horizontal yellow and red bands on the fly side and a green vertical band at the hoist. Adopted in 1959 to replace the French Tricolour, it was the flag of the Republic of Dahomey until 1975, when the People's Republic of Benin was established. The new regime renamed the country and changed the flag to a green field with a red star in the canton. This version was utilized until multi-party democracy was re-established in 1990, coinciding with the Revolutions of 1989. The new government promptly restored the original pre-1975 flag.",
  BFA: "Capital: Ouagadougou. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Burkinabe. Area: 272,967 km2 (rank 74 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13 N, 2 W. Population: about 23.0 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Tenakourou (749 m). National anthem: Une Seule Nuit. Official languages and their families: Dioula (language families: Mande, Niger–Congo); Bissa (language families: Mande, Niger–Congo); Mooré (language families: Atlantic–Congo, Gur, Oti–Volta, Niger–Congo, Volta–Congo). Form of government: republic, military junta. Lowest point: Black Volta. Internet domain: .bf. Calling code: +226. Flag: The national flag of Burkina Faso is formed by two equal horizontal bands of red (top) and green, with a yellow five-pointed star resting in the center. The flag was adopted on 4 August 1984. The flag uses the Pan-African colours of Ethiopia, reflecting both a break with the country's colonial past and its unity with other African ex-colonies. The red is also said to symbolize the revolution and the green the abundance of agricultural and natural riches. The yellow star placed over the red and green stripes represents the guiding light of the revolution. The flag was adopted following the coup of 1983 which brought Thomas Sankara to power.",
  BGD: "Capital: Dhaka. Currency: Bangladeshi taka (BDT). Languages listed as official or national: Bengali. People are called: Bangladeshi. Area: 147,570 km2 (rank 92 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 24 N, 90 E. Population: about 175.7 million (2025). Highest point: Mowdok tiang (1054 m). National anthem: Amar Sonar Bangla. Official languages and their families: Bangla (language families: Bengali–Assamese, Eastern Indo-Aryan, Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Form of government: Westminster system, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Bay of Bengal. Internet domain: .bd. Calling code: +880. Flag: The national flag of Bangladesh, nicknamed the Lal–Sobuj, was adopted officially on 17 January 1972. It consists of a red circle on top of a dark green field. The red circle is offset slightly toward the hoist so that it appears centred when the flag is flying. The civil ensign and naval ensign place it in the canton of a red or white field, respectively.",
  BGR: "Capital: Sofia. Currency: Bulgarian lev (BGN). Languages listed as official or national: Bulgarian. People are called: Bulgarian. Area: 110,879 km2 (rank 103 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 43 N, 25 E. Population: about 6.4 million (2025). Highest point: Musala (2925 m). National anthem: Mila Rodino. Official languages and their families: Bulgarian (language families: South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Black Sea. Internet domain: .bg. Calling code: +359. Flag: The national flag of the Republic of Bulgaria is a tricolour consisting of three equal-sized horizontal bands of white, green, and red. The flag was first adopted after the 1877–1878 Russo-Turkish War, when Bulgaria gained de facto independence. The national flag at times had as a supplement the state emblem, especially during the communist era. The current flag was re-established with the 1991 Constitution of Bulgaria and was confirmed in a 1998 law.",
  BHR: "Capital: Manama. Currency: Bahraini dinar (BHD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Bahraini. Area: 765 km2 (rank 173 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 26 N, 50.55 E. Population: about 1.6 million (2026). Highest point: Mountain of Smoke (134 m). National anthem: Bahrainona. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Persian Gulf. Internet domain: .bh. Calling code: +973. Flag: The flag of Bahrain consists of a white band on the left, separated from a red area on the right by five triangles that serve as a serrated line. The five white triangles symbolize the five pillars of Islam, the red on the flag represents the blood of the martyrs and the battles of Bahrain, and the white represents peace. It is sometimes mistaken for the flag of Qatar, but the latter flag is maroon, has nine points and normally has a much greater length-to-width ratio.",
  BHS: "Capital: Nassau. Currency: Bahamian dollar (BSD), United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Bahamian. Area: 13,943 km2 (rank 155 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 24.25 N, 76 W. Population: about 0.4 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Alvernia (63 m). National anthem: March On, Bahamaland. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .bs. Calling code: +1242. Flag: The national flag of the Commonwealth of The Bahamas consists of a black triangle situated at the hoist with three horizontal bands: aquamarine, gold and aquamarine. Adopted in 1973 to replace the British Blue Ensign defaced with the emblem of the Crown Colony of the Bahama Islands, it has been the flag of The Bahamas since the country gained independence that year. The design of the present flag incorporated the elements of various submissions made in a national contest for a new flag prior to independence.",
  BIH: "Capital: Sarajevo. Currency: Bosnia and Herzegovina convertible mark (BAM). Languages listed as official or national: Bosnian, Croatian, Serbian. People are called: Bosnian, Herzegovinian. Area: 51,209 km2 (rank 125 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 44 N, 18 E. Population: about 3.8 million (2022). Highest point: Maglić (2386 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Bosnia and Herzegovina. Official languages and their families: Bosnian (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic); Croatian (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic); Serbian (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: federal parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Adriatic Sea. Internet domain: .ba. Calling code: +387. Flag: The national flag of Bosnia and Herzegovina contains a blue field with a yellow right triangle separating said field, and there are seven full five-pointed white stars and two half stars, top and bottom, along the hypotenuse of the triangle.",
  BLR: "Capital: Minsk. Currency: Belarusian ruble (BYN). Languages listed as official or national: Belarusian, Russian. People are called: Belarusian. Area: 207,600 km2 (rank 84 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 53 N, 28 E. Population: about 9.1 million (2026). Highest point: Dziaržynkaja hara (345 m). National anthem: My Belarusy. Official languages and their families: Belarusian (language families: East Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic); Russian (language families: East Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Neman. Internet domain: .by. Calling code: +375. Flag: The national flag of Belarus is an unequal red-green bicolour with a red-on-white ornament pattern placed at the hoist (staff) end. The current design was introduced in 2012 by the State Committee for Standardisation of the Republic of Belarus, and is adapted from a design approved in a May 1995 referendum. It is a modification of the 1951 flag used while the country was a republic of the Soviet Union. Changes made to the Soviet-era flag were the removal of communist symbols – the hammer and sickle and the red star – as well as the reversal of the colours in the ornament pattern. Since the 1995 referendum, several flags used by Belarusian government officials and agencies have been modelled on this national flag.",
  BLZ: "Capital: Belmopan. Currency: Belize dollar (BZD). Languages listed as official or national: Belizean Creole, English, Spanish. People are called: Belizean. Area: 22,966 km2 (rank 147 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 17.25 N, 88.75 W. Population: about 0.4 million (2023). Highest point: Doyle's Delight (1124 m). National anthem: Land of the Free. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, parliamentary monarchy. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .bz. Calling code: +501. Flag: The flag of Belize was adopted on 21 September 1981, the day Belize became independent. It consists of the coat of arms of Belize on a blue field with red stripes at the top and bottom.",
  BOL: "Capital: Sucre. Currency: Bolivian boliviano (BOB). Languages listed as official or national: Aymara, Guaraní, Quechua, Spanish. People are called: Bolivian. Area: 1,098,581 km2 (rank 27 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 17 S, 65 W. Population: about 12.2 million (2023). Highest point: Nevado Sajama (6542 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Bolivia. Official languages and their families: Quechua (language families: Quechuan); Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Aymara (language families: Aymaran); Guarani (language families: Tupi–Guarani, Tupian). Form of government: republic, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Paraguay River. Internet domain: .bo. Calling code: +591. Flag: The original national flag of the Plurinational State of Bolivia was created in 1851. The state and war flag is a horizontal tricolor of red, yellow and green with the Bolivian coat of arms in the center, whereas the civil flag is just the three colors without the coat of arms.",
  BRA: "Capital: Brasília. Currency: Brazilian real (BRL). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese. People are called: Brazilian. Area: 8,515,767 km2 (rank 5 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 10 S, 55 W. Population: about 214.2 million (2026). Highest point: Pico da Neblina (2994 m). National anthem: Brazilian National Anthem. Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: presidential system, representative democracy, federal republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .br. Calling code: +55. Flag: The national flag of Brazil is a blue disc depicting a starry sky spanned by a curved band inscribed with the national motto Ordem e Progresso, within a yellow rhombus on a green field. It was officially adopted on 19 November 1889, four days after the Proclamation of the Republic, to replace the flag of the Empire of Brazil. The concept was the work of Raimundo Teixeira Mendes, with the collaboration of Miguel Lemos, Manuel Pereira Reis and Décio Villares.",
  BRB: "Capital: Bridgetown. Currency: Barbadian dollar (BBD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Barbadian. Area: 430 km2 (rank 183 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.16666666 N, 59.53333333 W. Population: about 0.3 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Hillaby (340 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Barbados. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Bajan Creole. Form of government: unitary state, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .bb. Calling code: +1246. Flag: The flag of Barbados was designed by Grantley W. Prescod and was officially adopted to represent Barbados at midnight on 30 November 1966, the day the country gained independence.",
  BRN: "Capital: Bandar Seri Begawan. Currency: Brunei dollar (BND), Singapore dollar (SGD). Languages listed as official or national: Malay. People are called: Bruneian. Area: 5,765 km2 (rank 164 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 4.5 N, 114.66666666 E. Population: about 0.5 million (2023). Highest point: Bukit Pagon (1850 m). National anthem: Allah Peliharakan Sultan. Official languages and their families: British English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, Germanic languages, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Malay (language families: Malayan, Malayic, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Form of government: unitary state, absolute monarchy, Islamic state. Lowest point: South China Sea. Internet domain: .bn. Calling code: +673. Flag: The flag of Brunei was originally a solid yellow background until 1906 when it added two diagonal white and black striped lines. At that point, Brunei had been a British protectorate since 1888. The modern flag was adopted in 1959 along with their new constitution.",
  BTN: "Capital: Thimphu. Currency: Bhutanese ngultrum (BTN), Indian rupee (INR). Languages listed as official or national: Dzongkha. People are called: Bhutanese. Area: 38,394 km2 (rank 133 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 27.5 N, 90.5 E. Population: about 0.8 million (2025). Highest point: Gangkhar Puensum (7570 m). National anthem: Druk Tsenden. Official languages and their families: Dzongkha (language families: Southern Tibetic, Bodish, Tibeto-Burman, Sino-Tibetan, Dené–Caucasian, Sino-Austronesian, Sino-Tibetan-Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Drangme Chhu. Internet domain: .bt. Calling code: +975. Flag: The national flag of Bhutan is one of the national symbols of Bhutan. The flag features the Druk, a dragon from Bhutanese mythology. This alludes to the country's name in Dzongkha: འབྲུག་ཡུལ་ Druk Yul, meaning 'The Thunder Dragon Kingdom', as well as the Drukpa Lineage of Tibetan Buddhism—the dominant religion of Bhutan.",
  BWA: "Capital: Gaborone. Currency: Botswana pula (BWP). Languages listed as official or national: English, Tswana. People are called: Motswana. Area: 582,000 km2 (rank 46 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 22 S, 24 E. Population: about 2.5 million (2023). Highest point: Otse Hill (1491 m). National anthem: Fatshe leno la rona. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Limpopo River. Internet domain: .bw. Calling code: +267. Flag: The national flag of Botswana consists of an azure blue background cut horizontally in the centre by a black stripe with a thin white frame. The flag's design is outlined in the Botswana Emblems Law, and Section 91 of the Botswana Penal Code criminalises insults towards the flag. The blue represents water and rainfall, and the black stripe with a white frame represents racial harmony. The flag was designed by George Winstanley, a British colonial administrator, and it was adopted in 1966 to replace the Union Jack following its independence from the United Kingdom.",
  CAF: "Capital: Bangui. Currency: Central African CFA franc (XAF). Languages listed as official or national: French, Sango. People are called: Central African. Area: 622,984 km2 (rank 42 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 7 N, 21 E. Population: about 5.2 million (2023). Highest point: Mont Ngaoui (1410 m). National anthem: La Renaissance. Official languages and their families: Sango (language families: Ubangian, Adamawa–Ubangi, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo, Atlantic–Congo); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Ubangi River. Internet domain: .cf. Calling code: +236. Flag: The flag of the Central African Republic was officially adopted in 1958. It has been retained since that time with the same design, four horizontal stripes of blue, white, green and yellow, and a single vertical band of red, with a yellow five-pointed star in the upper left corner.",
  CAN: "Capital: Ottawa. Currency: Canadian dollar (CAD). Languages listed as official or national: English, French. People are called: Canadian. Area: 9,984,670 km2 (rank 2 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 60 N, 95 W. Population: about 41.8 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Logan (5959 m). National anthem: O Canada. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, federation, parliamentary system. Lowest point: Arctic Ocean. Internet domain: .ca. Calling code: +1. Flag: The national flag of Canada, popularly referred to as the Maple Leaf, consists of a red field with a white square at its centre in the horizontal ratio of 1∶2∶1, in which is featured one stylized, red, 11-pointed maple leaf charged in the centre. It is the first flag to have been adopted by both houses of Parliament and officially proclaimed by the Canadian monarch as the country's official national flag. The flag has become the predominant and most recognizable national symbol of Canada.",
  CHE: "Capital: Bern. Currency: Swiss franc (CHF). Languages listed as official or national: French, Swiss German, Italian, Romansh. People are called: Swiss. Area: 41,284 km2 (rank 132 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 47 N, 8 E. Population: about 9.1 million (2025). Highest point: Dufourspitze (4634 m). National anthem: Swiss Psalm. Official languages and their families: German (language families: West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); Romansh (language families: Rhaeto-Romance, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Italian (language families: Italo-Romance, Italo-Dalmatian, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: federal republic, directorial system. Lowest point: Lake Maggiore. Internet domain: .ch. Calling code: +41. Flag: The national flag of Switzerland displays a white Greek cross in the centre of a red field. The white cross is known as the Swiss cross or the federal cross. Its arms are rectangular, with a ratio of height to width of 7:6 for the vertical arms and 6:7 for the horizontal arms. The size of the cross in relation to the field was set in 2017 as 5:8. The Swiss flag is square, instead of the rectangular shape typical for national flags.",
  CHL: "Capital: Santiago. Currency: Chilean peso (CLP). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Chilean. Area: 756,102 km2 (rank 37 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 30 S, 71 W. Highest point: Ojos del Salado (6893 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Chile. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: democratic republic. Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .cl. Calling code: +56. Flag: The flag of Chile consists of two equal-height horizontal bands of white and red, with a blue square the same height as the white band in the canton, which bears a white five-pointed star in its center. It was adopted on 18 October 1817. The Chilean flag is also known in Spanish as La Estrella Solitaria.",
  CHN: "Capital: Beijing. Currency: Chinese yuan (CNY). Languages listed as official or national: Chinese. People are called: Chinese. Area: 9,706,961 km2 (rank 3 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 35 N, 105 E. Population: about 1,404.9 million (2025). Highest point: Mount Everest (8849 m). National anthem: March of the Volunteers. Official languages and their families: Putonghua (language families: Sino-Tibetan, Dené–Caucasian, Sino-Austronesian, Sino-Tibetan-Indo-European). Form of government: people's republic. Lowest point: Ayding Lake. Internet domain: .cn. Calling code: +86. Flag: The national flag of the People's Republic of China is a Chinese red field with five golden stars charged at the canton. The design features one large star, with four smaller stars in an arc set off towards the fly. The five stars and their relationships to each other represent the unity of the Chinese people, symbolized by four smaller stars, under the Chinese Communist Party (CCP), symbolized by the large star. The red represents the Chinese Communist Revolution. It is commonly called the 'Five-star Red Flag' in China.",
  CIV: "Capital: Yamoussoukro. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Ivorian. Area: 322,463 km2 (rank 69 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 N, 5 W. Population: about 31.2 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Richard-Molard (1752 m). National anthem: L'Abidjanaise. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Gulf of Guinea. Internet domain: .ci. Calling code: +225. Flag: The national flag of Ivory Coast is a vertical tricolour of orange, white, and green, with a 2:3 width-to-length ratio. It was adopted on 3 December 1959 with the passing of law no. 59-240 by the Ivorian Legislative Assembly, and is defined in Article 48 of the country's 2016 constitution. It is similar to the flag of Ireland, but with the tricolour reversed and with less oblong proportions.",
  CMR: "Capital: Yaoundé. Currency: Central African CFA franc (XAF). Languages listed as official or national: English, French. People are called: Cameroonian. Area: 475,442 km2 (rank 53 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 6 N, 12 E. Population: about 28.4 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Cameroon (4095 m). National anthem: O Cameroon, Cradle of Our Forefathers. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Bight of Biafra. Internet domain: .cm. Calling code: +237. Flag: The national flag of Cameroon is a vertical tricolour of green, red and yellow pales, with one yellow five-pointed star in its center. This flag was originally adopted on 26 October 1957 as a plain tricolor then gained its present form on 20 May 1975 after Cameroon became a unitary state. There is a wide variation in the size of the central star, although it is always contained within the inside stripe.",
  COD: "Capital: Kinshasa. Currency: Congolese franc (CDF). Languages listed as official or national: French, Kikongo, Lingala, Tshiluba, Swahili. People are called: Congolese. Area: 2,344,858 km2 (rank 11 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 0 N, 25 E. Population: about 105.8 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Stanley (5109 m). National anthem: Debout Congolais. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .cd. Calling code: +243. Flag: The national flag of the Democratic Republic of the Congo is a sky blue flag, adorned with a yellow star in the upper left canton and cut diagonally by a red stripe with a yellow fimbriation. It was adopted on 18 February 2006. A new constitution, ratified in December 2005 and which came into effect in February 2006, promoted a return to a flag similar to that flown between 1966 and 1971, with a change from a royal blue to sky blue background. Blue represents peace. Red stands for 'the blood of the country's martyrs', yellow the country's wealth; and the star symbolises the future.",
  COG: "Capital: Brazzaville. Currency: Central African CFA franc (XAF). Languages listed as official or national: French, Kikongo, Lingala. People are called: Congolese. Area: 342,000 km2 (rank 64 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1 S, 15 E. Population: about 6.3 million (2024). Highest point: Mont Nabemba (1020 m). National anthem: La Congolaise. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .cg. Calling code: +242. Flag: The national flag of the Republic of the Congo consists of a yellow diagonal band divided diagonally from the lower hoist-side corner, with a green upper triangle and red lower triangle. Adopted in 1959 to replace the French Tricolour, it was the flag of the Republic of the Congo until 1970, when the People's Republic of the Congo was established. The new regime changed the flag to a red field with the emblem of the People's Republic in the canton. This version was utilized until the regime collapsed in 1991. The new government promptly restored the original pre-1970 flag.",
  COL: "Capital: Bogotá. Currency: Colombian peso (COP). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Colombian. Area: 1,141,748 km2 (rank 25 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 4 N, 72 W. Population: about 52.3 million (2023). Highest point: Pico Simón Bolívar (5775 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Colombia. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .co. Calling code: +57. Flag: The flag of the Republic of Colombia, also known as El Tricolor Nacional, is the national flag representing the country and, alongside the coat of arms and the national anthem, constitutes one of its official national symbols. The flag consists of a rectangle divided into three horizontal bands featuring the primary colors of the RYB color model. The upper band occupies half of the total height, following a proportional ratio of 2:1:1.",
  COM: "Capital: Moroni. Currency: Comorian franc (KMF). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, French, Comorian. People are called: Comoran. Area: 1,862 km2 (rank 170 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 12.16666666 S, 44.25 E. Population: about 0.9 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Karthala (2361 m). National anthem: Udzima wa ya Masiwa. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); Comorian (language families: Sabaki, Northeast Coast Bantu, Northeast Bantu, East Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .km. Calling code: +269. Flag: The national flag of the Union of the Comoros was designed in 2001 and officially adopted on 23 December of that year. It continues to display the crescent and four stars, which is a motif that has been in use in various forms since 1975 during the independence movement.",
  CPV: "Capital: Praia. Currency: Cape Verdean escudo (CVE). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese. People are called: Cape Verdian. Area: 4,033 km2 (rank 166 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 16 N, 24 W. Highest point: Pico do Fogo (2829 m). National anthem: Cântico da Liberdade. Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Cape Verdean Creole. Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Pedra de Lume salt ponds. Internet domain: .cv. Calling code: +238. Flag: The national flag of Cape Verde consists of five horizontal bands of blue, white, red, white, and blue, in a 6:1:1:1:3 ratio. A circle of ten yellow five-pointed stars – representing the ten islands that make up the country – is charged on the hoist (left) side of the flag, centred on the red band. The current Cape Verdean flag was adopted on 25 September 1992, replacing the flag that was adopted when the country became independent on 5 July 1975.",
  CRI: "Capital: San José. Currency: Costa Rican colón (CRC). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Costa Rican. Area: 51,100 km2 (rank 126 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 10 N, 84 W. Population: about 5.3 million (2024). Highest point: Cerro Chirripó (3820 m). National anthem: Noble patria, tu hermosa bandera. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .cr. Calling code: +506. Flag: The national flag of Costa Rica is based on a design created in 1848 and consists of two blue stripes, two white stripes, and a central red stripe which is twice as wide as each of the other four. The civil flag omits the coat of arms seen on the state flag, since the state variant is only permitted to be used by the government.",
  CUB: "Capital: Havana. Currency: Cuban convertible peso (CUC), Cuban peso (CUP). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Cuban. Area: 109,884 km2 (rank 104 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 21.5 N, 80 W. Population: about 11.0 million (2023). Highest point: Pico Turquino (1974 m). National anthem: El Himno de Bayamo. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: unitary state. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .cu. Calling code: +53. Flag: The national flag of Cuba, also known as the Flag of the Lone Star, consists of five alternating stripes and a cherry red triangle at the hoist, within which is a white five-pointed star. It was designed in 1849 and officially adopted May 20, 1902. The flag is referred to as the Estrella Solitaria, or the Lone Star flag. Vexillologist Alfred Znamierowski assigns the flag to his Stars and Stripes flag family.",
  CYP: "Capital: Nicosia. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Greek, Turkish. People are called: Cypriot. Area: 9,251 km2 (rank 162 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 35 N, 33 E. Population: about 1.3 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Olympus (1952 m). National anthem: Hymn to Liberty. Official languages and their families: Turkish (language families: Western Oghuz, Oghuz, Common Turkic languages, Turkic); Modern Greek (language families: Greek, Hellenic, Indo-European, Graeco-Phrygian). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Mediterranean Sea. Internet domain: .cy. Calling code: +357. Flag: The flag of Cyprus came into use on 16 August 1960, under the Zürich and London Agreements, whereby a constitution was drafted and Cyprus was proclaimed an independent state. The flag was designed by Turkish Cypriot artist İsmet Güney. The design of the flag deliberately employs peaceful and neutral symbols in an attempt to indicate harmony between the Greek and Turkish Cypriot communities, an ideal that has not yet been realised. In 1963, Greek Cypriot and Turkish Cypriot communities separated because of Cypriot intercommunal violence.",
  CZE: "Capital: Prague. Currency: Czech koruna (CZK). Languages listed as official or national: Czech, Slovak. People are called: Czech. Area: 78,865 km2 (rank 115 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 49.75 N, 15.5 E. Population: about 10.9 million (2025). Highest point: Sněžka (1603 m). National anthem: Kde domov můj. Official languages and their families: Czech (language families: West Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Elbe. Internet domain: .cz. Calling code: +420. Flag: The national flag of the Czech Republic is the same as the flag of the former Czechoslovakia. Upon the dissolution of Czechoslovakia in December 1992, the Czech Republic kept the Czechoslovak flag while Slovakia adopted its own flag. The first flag of Czechoslovakia was based on the flag of Bohemia and was white over red. This was almost identical to the flag of Poland officially adopted in 1919, so a blue triangle was added at the hoist in 1920. The flag was banned by the Nazis in 1939 as they established a government nominally in control of Bohemia and Moravia, and a horizontal tricolour of white, red, and blue was used for the duration of the war. The 1920–1939 flag was restored in 1945. The flag was one of the only three flags of a socialist country in Europe without its coat of arms placed on center, when it was still part of Czechoslovakia at that time.",
  DEU: "Capital: Berlin. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: German. People are called: German. Area: 357,114 km2 (rank 62 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 51 N, 9 E. Population: about 83.5 million (2025). Highest point: Zugspitze (2962 m). National anthem: third stanza of the Deutschlandlied. Official languages and their families: German (language families: West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: federal parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Neuendorf-Sachsenbande. Internet domain: .de. Calling code: +49. Flag: The national flag of Germany is a tricolour consisting of three equal horizontal bands displaying the national colours of Germany: black, red, and gold. The flag was first sighted in 1848 in the German Confederation. The flag was also used by the German Empire from 1848 to 1849. It was officially adopted as the national flag of the German Reich from 1919 to 1933, and has been in use since its reintroduction in the Federal Republic of Germany in 1949.",
  DJI: "Capital: Djibouti. Currency: Djiboutian franc (DJF). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, French. People are called: Djibouti. Area: 23,200 km2 (rank 146 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 11.5 N, 43 E. Population: about 1.0 million (2026). Highest point: Mousa Ali (2028 m). National anthem: Djibouti. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Lake Assal. Internet domain: .dj. Calling code: +253. Flag: The national flag of Djibouti is a horizontal flag bicolour with equal bands of light blue and light green, with a white, equilateral triangle at the hoist. In the centre of the triangle is a red star. The flag combines the basic layout and colours from the flag of the Front de Libération de la Côte des Somalis. The light blue represents the sky and the sea, as well as the Somalis, green represents the everlasting green of the earth, as well as the Afars, white represents the colour of peace and the five-pointed red star represents unity, the blood shed by the martyrs of independence, as well as Djibouti being one of the five regions inhabited by the Somali people.",
  DMA: "Capital: Roseau. Currency: Eastern Caribbean dollar (XCD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Dominican. Area: 751 km2 (rank 174 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15.41666666 N, 61.33333333 W. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: Morne Diablotins (1447 m). National anthem: Isle of Beauty, Isle of Splendour. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .dm. Calling code: +1767. Flag: The national flag of Dominica was adopted on 3 November 1978, with some small changes having been made in 1981, 1988, and 1990. The original flag was designed by playwright Alwin Bully in early 1978 as the country prepared for independence.",
  DNK: "Capital: Copenhagen. Currency: Danish krone (DKK). Languages listed as official or national: Danish. People are called: Danish. Area: 43,094 km2 (rank 130 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 56 N, 10 E. Highest point: Møllehøj (171 m). National anthem: Der er et yndigt land. Official languages and their families: Danish (language families: East Scandinavian, North Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Lammefjord. Internet domain: .dk. Calling code: +45. Flag: The Dannebrog is the flag of the Kingdom of Denmark. The flag is red with a white Nordic cross, which means that the cross extends to the edges of the flag and that the vertical part of the cross is shifted to the hoist side.",
  DOM: "Capital: Santo Domingo. Currency: Dominican peso (DOP). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Dominican. Area: 48,671 km2 (rank 128 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 19 N, 70.66666666 W. Population: about 10.8 million (2022). Highest point: Pico Duarte (3101 m). National anthem: National Anthem of the Dominican Republic. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Lowest point: Lake Enriquillo. Internet domain: .do. Calling code: +1. Flag: The national flag of the Dominican Republic is one of the official national symbols of the nation, along with the coat of arms and the national anthem. The blue on the flag stands for liberty, the white for salvation, and the red for the blood of heroes. The civil flag follows the same design, but without the charge in the center, which is the coat of arms of the Dominican Republic. The flag was designed by Juan Pablo Duarte.",
  DZA: "Capital: Algiers. Currency: Algerian dinar (DZD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Algerian. Area: 2,381,741 km2 (rank 10 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 28 N, 3 E. Population: about 45.6 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Tahat (2918 m). National anthem: Kassaman. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); Standard Algerian Berber (language families: Berber, Northern Berber languages, Afroasiatic). Form of government: semi-presidential system, people's republic. Lowest point: Chott Melrhir. Internet domain: .dz. Calling code: +213. Flag: The national flag of Algeria consists of two equal vertical bars, green and white, charged in the center with a red star and crescent, a symbol of Islam as the nation's prominent faith. The flag was adopted on 3 July 1962. A similar version was used by the Algerian government in exile from 1958 to 1962. The Western blazon is per pale vert and argent; a crescent and star gules.",
  ECU: "Capital: Quito. Currency: United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Ecuadorean. Area: 276,841 km2 (rank 73 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 2 S, 77.5 W. Population: about 16.9 million (2022). Highest point: Chimborazo (6263 m). National anthem: Salve, Oh Patria. Official languages and their families: Shuar (language families: Chicham, Jivaroan-Cahuapanan); Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Northern Quichua (language families: Northern Quechua, Quechua II, Quechua, Quechuan). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .ec. Calling code: +593. Flag: The national flag of Ecuador, which consists of horizontal bands of the Pan-Colombian colors of yellow, blue and red, was first adopted by law in 1835 and later on 26 September 1860. The design of the current flag was finalized in 1900 with the addition of the coat of arms in the center of the flag. Before using the yellow, blue and red tricolor, Ecuador's former flag had three light blue stripes and two white stripes with three white stars for each province of the country. The design of the flag is very similar to those of Colombia and Venezuela, which are also former constituent territories of Gran Colombia. All three are based on a proposal by Venezuelan General Francisco de Miranda, which was adopted by Venezuela in 1811 and later Gran Colombia with some modifications. A variant of the flag that does not contain the coat of arms is used by the merchant marine; this flag matches Colombia's in every aspect, but Colombia uses a different design when the merchant marine ships are at sail.",
  EGY: "Capital: Cairo. Currency: Egyptian pound (EGP). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Egyptian. Area: 1,002,450 km2 (rank 29 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 27 N, 30 E. Population: about 114.5 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Catherine (2629 m). National anthem: Bilady, Bilady, Bilady. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Qattara Depression. Internet domain: .eg. Calling code: +20. Flag: The national flag of Egypt is a tricolour consisting of the three equal horizontal red, white, and black bands of the Arab Liberation Flag that dates back to the 1952 Egyptian Revolution. The flag bears Egypt's national emblem, the Egyptian eagle of Saladin, centred in the white band.",
  ERI: "Capital: Asmara. Currency: Eritrean nakfa (ERN). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, English, Tigrinya. People are called: Eritrean. Area: 117,600 km2 (rank 99 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 N, 39 E. Highest point: Emba Soira (3013 m). National anthem: Ertra, Ertra, Ertra. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); Tigrinya (language families: Semitic, Ethiopian Semitic, Afroasiatic, South Semitic, West Semitic). Lowest point: Lake Kulul. Internet domain: .er. Calling code: +291. Flag: The flag of Eritrea is the national flag of the State of Eritrea. It was adopted on 5 December 1995. The flag combines the basic layout and colors from the flag of the Eritrean People's Liberation Front with an emblem of a wreath and an upright olive branch derived from the Eritrean flag from 1952 to 1962.",
  ESP: "Capital: Madrid. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Spanish. Area: 505,992 km2 (rank 51 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 40 N, 4 W. Population: about 48.6 million (2024). Highest point: Teide (3715 m). Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: parliamentary monarchy. Lowest point: Mina de Las Cruces. Internet domain: .es. Calling code: +34. Flag: The national flag of Spain, as it is defined in the Constitution of 1978, consists of three horizontal lines: red, yellow and red, the yellow stripe being twice the height of each red stripe. Traditionally, the middle stripe color was called by the archaic term gualda ; hence the flag's nickname la Rojigualda. The middle stripe bears the coat of arms of Spain, being mandatory in several cases.",
  EST: "Capital: Tallinn. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Estonian. People are called: Estonian. Area: 45,227 km2 (rank 129 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 59 N, 26 E. Population: about 1.4 million (2025). Highest point: Suur Munamägi (317 m). National anthem: Mu isamaa, mu õnn ja rõõm. Official languages and their families: Estonian (language families: Finnic, Uralic, Finno-Samic, Finno-Volgaic, Finno-Permic, Finno-Ugric). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Baltic Sea. Internet domain: .ee. Calling code: +372. Flag: The national flag of Estonia is a tricolour featuring three equal horizontal bands of blue at the top, black in the middle, and white at the bottom. The flag is called sinimustvalge in Estonian.",
  ETH: "Capital: Addis Ababa. Currency: Ethiopian birr (ETB). Languages listed as official or national: Amharic. People are called: Ethiopian. Area: 1,104,300 km2 (rank 26 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 N, 38 E. Population: about 128.7 million (2023). Highest point: Ras Dashen (4533 m). National anthem: March Forward, Dear Mother Ethiopia. Official languages and their families: Amharic (language families: Semitic, Ethiopian Semitic, Afroasiatic, South Semitic, West Semitic). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Danakil Depression. Internet domain: .et. Calling code: +251. Flag: The flag of Ethiopia consists of a green, yellow, and red tricolour with the national emblem, a golden pentagram on a blue disc, superimposed at the centre. While the colours green, yellow, and red in combination held symbolic importance since at least the early 17th century, the modern tricolour was first adopted on 11 October 1897 by Menelik II, and the present flag on 31 October 1996.",
  FIN: "Capital: Helsinki. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Finnish, Swedish. People are called: Finnish. Area: 338,424 km2 (rank 65 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 64 N, 26 E. Population: about 5.7 million (2025). Highest point: Halti (1361 m). National anthem: Maamme. Official languages and their families: Swedish (language families: East Scandinavian, North Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Finnish (language families: Finnic, Finno-Samic, Finno-Volgaic, Finno-Permic, Finno-Ugric, Uralic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Baltic Sea. Internet domain: .fi. Calling code: +358. Flag: The national flag of Finland, also known in Finnish as the siniristilippu, dates from the beginning of the 20th century. The flag was adopted after independence from the Russian Empire, but its design has roots in the 19th century.",
  FJI: "Capital: Suva. Currency: Fijian dollar (FJD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Fijian, Fiji Hindi. People are called: Fijian. Area: 18,272 km2 (rank 151 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 18 S, 175 E. Highest point: Mount Tomanivi (1324 m). National anthem: God Bless Fiji. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Fijian (language families: East Fijian, Central Pacific, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); Fiji Hindi (language families: Central Indo-Aryan, Western Hindi, Indo-European, Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .fj. Calling code: +679. Flag: The national flag of Fiji was adopted on 10 October 1970. The state arms have been slightly modified but the flag has remained the same as during Fiji's colonial period. It is a defaced cyan 'Blue Ensign', with the shield from the national coat of arms. It has remained unchanged since Fiji was declared a republic in 1987, despite calls from some politicians for changes.",
  FRA: "Capital: Paris. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: French. Area: 551,695 km2 (rank 48 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 46 N, 2 E. Population: about 68.6 million (2025). Highest point: Mont Blanc (4806 m). National anthem: La Marseillaise. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: semi-presidential system. Lowest point: Étang de Lavalduc. Internet domain: .fr. Calling code: +33. Flag: The national flag of France is a tricolour featuring three vertical bands coloured blue, white, and red. The design was adopted during the French Revolution and has remained the national flag since then, with only minor variations in shade and proportion. The tricolour scheme was later adopted by many other nations in Europe and elsewhere, and, according to the Encyclopædia Britannica, has historically stood 'in symbolic opposition to the autocratic and clericalist royal standards of the past'.",
  FSM: "Capital: Palikir. Currency: . Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Micronesian. Area: 702 km2 (rank 177 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 6.91666666 N, 158.25 E. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: Dolohmwar (791 m). National anthem: Patriots of Micronesia. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .fm. Calling code: +691. Flag: The flag of the Federated States of Micronesia was adopted on 30 November 1978. The blue field represents the Pacific Ocean. In an echo of U.S. heraldic practice, the stars represent the four federated states: Chuuk, Pohnpei, Kosrae and Yap, arranged like the points of the compass. It is based on the flag of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands.",
  GAB: "Capital: Libreville. Currency: Central African CFA franc (XAF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Gabonese. Area: 267,668 km2 (rank 76 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1 S, 11.75 E. Population: about 2.3 million (2023). Highest point: Mont Bengoué (1070 m). National anthem: La Concorde. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .ga. Calling code: +241. Flag: The flag of Gabon is a tricolour consisting of three horizontal green, yellow, and blue bands. Adopted in 1960 to replace the previous colonial flag containing the French Tricolour at the canton, it has been the flag of the Gabonese Republic since the country gained independence that year. The design of the present flag entailed the removal of the Tricolour and the widening of the yellow stripe at the centre.",
  GBR: "Capital: London. Currency: British pound (GBP). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: British. Area: 242,900 km2 (rank 78 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 54 N, 2 W. Highest point: Ben Nevis (1345 m). National anthem: God Save the King. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: The Fens. Internet domain: .uk. Calling code: +44. Flag: The Union Jack or Union Flag is the national flag of the United Kingdom. While no law has been enacted making the Union Flag the national flag of the United Kingdom, it has become so through precedent.",
  GEO: "Capital: Tbilisi. Currency: lari (GEL). Languages listed as official or national: Georgian. People are called: Georgian. Area: 69,700 km2 (rank 119 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 42 N, 43.5 E. Population: about 3.9 million (2024). Highest point: Shkhara (5193 m). National anthem: Tavisupleba. Official languages and their families: Georgian (language families: Karto-Zan, Kartvelian, Ibero-Caucasian). Form of government: semi-presidential system, presidential system, unitary state, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Black Sea. Internet domain: .ge. Calling code: +995. Flag: Flag of Georgia may refer to:Flag of Georgia (country) Flag of the Georgian Soviet Socialist Republic Flag of Georgia",
  GHA: "Capital: Accra. Currency: Ghanaian cedi (GHS). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Ghanaian. Area: 238,533 km2 (rank 80 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 N, 2 W. Highest point: Mount Afadja (885 m). National anthem: God Bless Our Homeland Ghana. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: democracy. Lowest point: Gulf of Guinea. Internet domain: .gh. Calling code: +233. Flag: The national flag of Ghana consists of a horizontal triband of red, yellow, and green with a black five-pointed star in the center taking up all of the width of the yellow stripe, touching the red and green stripes. It replaced the British Gold Coast's Blue Ensign.",
  GIN: "Capital: Conakry. Currency: Guinean franc (GNF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Guinean. Area: 245,857 km2 (rank 77 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 11 N, 10 W. Population: about 17.5 million (2025). Highest point: Mount Richard-Molard (1752 m). National anthem: Liberté. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); languages of Guinea. Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .gn. Calling code: +224. Flag: The national flag of Guinea is a triband of red, yellow, and green. It was adopted on 10 November 1958, with the publication of the country's first Constitution. It uses the Pan-African colours, partly as a nod to the flag of Ghana, whose president was a close associate of Sekou Toure, the first Guinean president. It is a tricolour resembling the flag of France, its former coloniser.",
  GMB: "Capital: Banjul. Currency: dalasi (GMD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Gambian. Area: 10,689 km2 (rank 160 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.46666666 N, 16.56666666 W. National anthem: For The Gambia Our Homeland. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .gm. Calling code: +220. Flag: The national flag of The Gambia consists of three horizontal red, blue and green bands separated by two thin white stripes. Adopted in 1965 to replace the British Blue Ensign defaced with the arms of the Gambia Colony and Protectorate, it has been the flag of the Republic of the Gambia since the country gained independence that year. It remained unchanged throughout the Gambia's seven-year confederation with Senegal.",
  GNB: "Capital: Bissau. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese, Upper Guinea Creole. People are called: Guinea-Bissauan. Area: 36,125 km2 (rank 134 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 12 N, 15 W. Highest point: Monte Torin (262 m). National anthem: Esta é a Nossa Pátria bem Amada. Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .gw. Calling code: +245. Flag: The national flag of Guinea-Bissau was adopted on the day Guinea-Bissau proclaimed its independence from Portugal on 24 September 1973. It is almost identical to the flag of the African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde, the country's dominant party and previously sole ruling party.",
  GNQ: "Capital: Malabo. Currency: Central African CFA franc (XAF). Languages listed as official or national: French, Portuguese, Spanish. People are called: Equatorial Guinean. Area: 28,051 km2 (rank 141 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 2 N, 10 E. Population: about 1.8 million (2023). Highest point: Pico Basilé (3011 m). National anthem: Caminemos pisando las sendas de nuestra inmensa felicidad. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .gq. Calling code: +240. Flag: The national flag of Equatorial Guinea is a horizontal tricolour of green, white, and red with a blue isosceles triangle based at the hoist and the coat of arms of Equatorial Guinea centered on the white band. The flag without the coat of arms was raised upon the country's independence from Spain on 12 October 1968. While both the flag and coat of arms were adopted at independence, the coat of arms was added to the flag later on. The coat of arms and accordingly the national flag were briefly changed from 1978 to 21 August 1979, during the presidency of Francisco Macías Nguema.",
  GRC: "Capital: Athens. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Greek. People are called: Greek. Area: 131,990 km2 (rank 95 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 39 N, 22 E. Population: about 10.6 million (2022). Highest point: Mount Olympus (2918 m). National anthem: Hymn to Liberty. Official languages and their families: Greek (language families: Hellenic, Indo-European, Graeco-Phrygian). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Calypso Deep. Internet domain: .gr. Calling code: +30. Flag: The national flag of Greece, popularly referred to as the Blue-and-White or the Cyan-and-White, is officially recognised by Greece as one of its national symbols and has 9 horizontal stripes of alternating blue and white. There is a blue canton in the upper hoist-side corner bearing a white cross; the cross symbolises Christianity. The blazon of the flag is azure, four bars argent; on a canton of the field a Greek cross throughout of the second. The shade of blue used in the flag has varied throughout its history, from light blue to dark blue, the latter being increasingly used since the late 1960s. It was officially adopted by the First National Assembly at Epidaurus on 13 January 1822.",
  GRD: "Capital: St. George's. Currency: Eastern Caribbean dollar (XCD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Grenadian. Area: 344 km2 (rank 185 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 12.11666666 N, 61.66666666 W. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Saint Catherine (840 m). National anthem: Hail Grenada. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Grenadian Creole English. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .gd. Calling code: +1473. Flag: The flag of Grenada consists of two yellow triangles at the top and bottom and two green triangles at the hoist and fly. These are surrounded by a red border charged with six five-pointed yellow stars – three at the top centre and three at the bottom centre – along with an additional star on a red disc at the centre and a nutmeg at the hoist triangle. Adopted in 1974 to replace the temporary design used since the islands became an Associated State of the United Kingdom, it has been the flag of Grenada since the country gained independence that year. The representation of a nutmeg is symbolic of the islands' primary export, and was the one feature from the previous flag that was preserved.",
  GTM: "Capital: Guatemala City. Currency: Guatemalan quetzal (GTQ). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Guatemalan. Area: 108,889 km2 (rank 105 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15.5 N, 90.25 W. Highest point: Volcán Tajumulco (4203 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Guatemala. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: presidential system, representative democracy. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .gt. Calling code: +502. Flag: The flag of Guatemala, often referred to as the National Pavilion or the Blue-and-White, features two colors: sky blue and white. According to decree, the two sky blue stripes represents strength, justice, truth and loyalty. The white color signifies purity, integrity, firmness and light. The blue and white colors, like those of several other countries in the region, are based on the flag of the former Federal Republic of Central America.",
  GUY: "Capital: Georgetown. Currency: Guyanese dollar (GYD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Guyanese. Area: 214,969 km2 (rank 83 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 5 N, 59 W. Population: about 0.9 million (2022). Highest point: Mount Roraima (2810 m). National anthem: National anthem of Guyana. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .gy. Calling code: +592. Flag: The national flag of Guyana, known as the Golden Arrowhead, was adopted on May 1966, when the country became independent from the United Kingdom. It was designed by Whitney Smith, an American vexillologist. The proportions of the national flag are 3:5.",
  HND: "Capital: Tegucigalpa. Currency: Honduran lempira (HNL). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Honduran. Area: 112,492 km2 (rank 101 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 N, 86.5 W. Population: about 11.0 million (2025). Highest point: Cerro Las Minas (2849 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Honduras. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .hn. Calling code: +504. Flag: The flag of Honduras consists of three equal horizontal stripes of navy blue, white and the same shade of blue, with five blue stars arranged in a quincuncial pattern at the centre of the middle stripe. The two outer bands represent the Pacific Ocean and the Caribbean Sea, and also represent the blue sky and brotherhood. The inner band represents the land between the ocean and the sea, the peace and prosperity of its people, and purity of thoughts. The five stars represent the five nations of the former Federal Republic of Central America and the hope that the nations may form a union again.",
  HRV: "Capital: Zagreb. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Croatian. People are called: Croatian. Area: 56,594 km2 (rank 124 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 45.16666666 N, 15.5 E. Highest point: Dinara (1831 m). National anthem: Lijepa naša domovino. Official languages and their families: Croatian (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Adriatic Sea. Internet domain: .hr. Calling code: +385. Flag: The national flag of the Republic of Croatia, also known in Croatian as the Tricolor or the Red-white-blue (Crven-bijeli-plavi), is one of the state symbols of Croatia. It consists of three equal size, horizontal stripes in colors red, white and blue anchored by the coat of arms of Croatia.",
  HTI: "Capital: Port-au-Prince. Currency: Haitian gourde (HTG). Languages listed as official or national: French, Haitian Creole. People are called: Haitian. Area: 27,750 km2 (rank 143 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 19 N, 72.41666666 W. Highest point: Peak la Selle (2674 m). National anthem: La Dessalinienne. Official languages and their families: Haitian Creole (language families: French-based creole languages); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .ht. Calling code: +509. Flag: The flag of Haiti is a bicolour featuring two horizontal bands coloured blue and red, emblazoned by a white rectangular panel bearing the coat of arms of Haiti. The coat of arms depicts a trophy of weapons atop a green hill and a royal palm symbolizing independence. The palm is topped by the Cap of Liberty. The motto L'Union fait la Force appears on a white ribbon below the arrangement.",
  HUN: "Capital: Budapest. Currency: Hungarian forint (HUF). Languages listed as official or national: Hungarian. People are called: Hungarian. Area: 93,028 km2 (rank 108 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 47 N, 20 E. Population: about 9.5 million (2025). Highest point: Kékes (1014 m). National anthem: Himnusz. Official languages and their families: Hungarian (language families: Ugric, Uralic, Finno-Ugric). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Gyálarét. Internet domain: .hu. Calling code: +36. Flag: The national flag of Hungary, also known as the Red-White-Green, is an equal horizontal tricolour of red, white and green. In this exact form, it has been the official flag of Hungary since 23 May 1957. The flag's form originates from national republican movements of the 18th and 19th centuries, while its colours are from the Middle Ages. The current Hungarian flag is similar to the 1816 version of the British republican tricolour, differing only in the shades used. The colours in that form were also already used since at least the coronation of Leopold II in 1790, predating the first use of the Cispadane Republic's tricolour in 1797. Following the removal of the coat of arms in 1957, it became one of only three flags of a socialist state in Europe to not incorporate socialist symbolism, alongside the Polish and Czechoslovak flags.",
  IDN: "Capital: Jakarta. Currency: Indonesian rupiah (IDR). Languages listed as official or national: Indonesian. People are called: Indonesian. Area: 1,904,569 km2 (rank 14 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 5 S, 120 E. Highest point: Puncak Jaya (4884 m). National anthem: Indonesia Raya. Official languages and their families: Indonesian (language families: Malayan, Malayic, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .id. Calling code: +62. Flag: The national flag of Indonesia is bicolor, with two horizontal bands, red (top) and white (bottom) with an overall ratio of 2:3. It was introduced and hoisted in public during the proclamation of independence on 17 August 1945 at 56 Jalan Proklamasi in Jakarta, and again when the Dutch formally transferred sovereignty on 27 December 1949. The design of the flag has remained unchanged since. The flag is named Sang Saka Merah Putih, lit. 'The Heirloom Red-White'.",
  IND: "Capital: New Delhi. Currency: Indian rupee (INR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Hindi, Tamil. People are called: Indian. Area: 3,287,590 km2 (rank 7 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 20 N, 77 E. Highest point: Kanchenjunga (8586 m). National anthem: Jana Gana Mana. Official languages and their families: Hindi (language families: Western Hindi, Central Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian, Indo-Aryan, Indo-European); English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: republic, federal republic, constitutional republic, democratic republic. Lowest point: Kuttanad. Internet domain: .in. Calling code: +91. Flag: The national flag of India, colloquially called Tiraṅgā, is a horizontal rectangular tricolour flag, the colours being of India saffron, white and India green; with the Ashoka Chakra, a 24-spoke wheel, in navy blue at its centre. It was adopted in its present form during a meeting of the Constituent Assembly held on 22 July 1947, and it became the official flag of the Union of India on 15 August 1947. The flag was subsequently retained as that of the Republic of India. In India, the term 'tricolour' almost always refers to the Indian national flag.",
  IRL: "Capital: Dublin. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Irish. People are called: Irish. Area: 70,273 km2 (rank 118 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 53 N, 8 W. Population: about 5.1 million (2022). Highest point: Carrauntoohil (1039 m). National anthem: Amhrán na bhFiann. Official languages and their families: Irish (language families: Goidelic, Insular Celtic, Celtic languages, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: unitary state, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: North Slob. Internet domain: .ie. Calling code: +353. Flag: The national flag of Ireland, frequently referred to in Ireland as 'the tricolour' and elsewhere as the Irish tricolour, is a vertical tricolour of green, white and orange. The proportions of the flag are 1:2.",
  IRN: "Capital: Tehran. Currency: Iranian rial (IRR). Languages listed as official or national: Persian (Farsi). People are called: Iranian. Area: 1,648,195 km2 (rank 17 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 32 N, 53 E. Population: about 92.4 million (2025). Highest point: Damavand (5610 m). National anthem: National Anthem of the Islamic Republic of Iran. Official languages and their families: Persian (language families: Southwestern Iranian, Western Iranian, Iranian, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Form of government: presidential system, unitary state, Islamic Republic, islamic theocracy. Lowest point: Caspian Sea. Internet domain: .ir. Calling code: +98. Flag: The national flag of the Islamic Republic of Iran is a tricolour of equal horizontal bands of green, white, and red, featuring the Islamic emblem in red centred on the white band, and the Takbir written 11 times each in white Kufic script along the edges of the green and red bands.",
  IRQ: "Capital: Baghdad. Currency: Iraqi dinar (IQD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, Aramaic, Sorani. People are called: Iraqi. Area: 438,317 km2 (rank 58 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 33 N, 44 E. Population: about 43.5 million (2026). Highest point: Cheekha Dar (3611 m). National anthem: Mawtini. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); Kurdish language (language families: Iranian, Northwestern Iranian, Western Iranian, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Form of government: federation, federal republic. Internet domain: .iq. Calling code: +964. Flag: Since the 1958 Iraqi coup d'état, the various republican governments of Iraq have used a number of different flags, all featuring the pan-Arab colors of green, black, white, and red. The current official and internationally recognized flag of Iraq was adopted in 2008 as a temporary compromise, and consists of the three equal horizontal red, white, and black stripes of the Arab Liberation Flag, that was first used by Gamal Abdel Nasser during the Egyptian Revolution, with the takbīr written in green in the Kufic script that was originally added by Saddam Hussein following the Gulf War.",
  ISL: "Capital: Reykjavik. Currency: Icelandic króna (ISK). Languages listed as official or national: Icelandic. People are called: Icelander. Area: 103,000 km2 (rank 106 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 65 N, 18 W. Population: about 0.4 million (2022). Highest point: Hvannadalshnúkur (2110 m). National anthem: Lofsöngur. Official languages and their families: Icelandic (language families: West Scandinavian, North Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .is. Calling code: +354. Flag: The flag of Iceland is defined in Law No. 34/1944, adopted on 17 June 1944, the day Iceland became a republic. The law, entitled The Law of the National Flag of Icelanders and the State Arms, describes the flag as follows:The civil national flag of Icelanders is sky-blue with a snow-white cross and a fiery-red cross inside the white cross. The arms of the crosses extend to the edges of the flag and their width is 2⁄9, and the red cross 1⁄9 of the width of the flag. The blue sections are rectangles: the hoist sections are equilateral, and the outer sections are the same width as them but twice as long. The ratio between the width and length of the flag is 18:25.",
  ISR: "Capital: Jerusalem. Currency: Israeli new shekel (ILS). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, Hebrew. People are called: Israeli. Area: 20,770 km2 (rank 149 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 31.47 N, 35.13 E. Population: about 9.8 million (2023). Highest point: Meron (1208 m). National anthem: Hatikvah. Official languages and their families: Hebrew (language families: Canaanite, Northwest Semitic, Central Semitic, West Semitic, Semitic, Afroasiatic). Form of government: unitary state, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Dead Sea. Internet domain: .il. Calling code: +972. Flag: The flag of Israel was officially adopted on 28 October 1948. It is a white banner with three blue (tekhelet) symbols: a pair of horizontal tallit-like stripes above and below a centred Star of David. Relevant Israeli legislation describes the flag's dimensions as 160 cm (63 in) by 220 cm (87 in), thereby fixing the proportion to a ratio of 8:11. But variants can be found at a wide range of proportions, with 2:3 also common.",
  ITA: "Capital: Rome. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Italian. People are called: Italian. Area: 301,336 km2 (rank 72 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 42.83333333 N, 12.83333333 E. Population: about 58.9 million (2023). Highest point: Mont Blanc (4806 m). National anthem: Il Canto degli Italiani. Official languages and their families: Italian (language families: Italo-Romance, Italo-Dalmatian, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Contane. Internet domain: .it. Calling code: +39. Flag: The national flag of Italy, often referred to as the Tricolour, is a flag featuring three equally sized vertical pales of green, white and red, with the green at the hoist side, as defined by Article 12 of the Constitution of the Italian Republic. The Italian law regulates its use and display, protecting its defence and providing for the crime of insulting it; it also prescribes its teaching in Italian schools together with other national symbols of Italy.",
  JAM: "Capital: Kingston. Currency: Jamaican dollar (JMD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Jamaican Patois. People are called: Jamaican. Area: 10,991 km2 (rank 159 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 18.25 N, 77.5 W. Highest point: Blue Mountain Peak (2256 m). National anthem: God Save the King. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Jamaican Patois. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .jm. Calling code: +1876. Flag: The flag of Jamaica was adopted on 6 August 1962, the day Jamaica became independent from the United Kingdom. The flag consists of a gold saltire, which divides the flag into four sections: two of them green and two black. Jamaica's national flag is also sometimes referred to as 'The Cross' or as the 'Black, Green, and Gold' due to its form and colours. The flag of Jamaica has been said to have been directly inspired by the national flag of Scotland.",
  JOR: "Capital: Amman. Currency: Jordanian dinar (JOD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Jordanian. Area: 89,342 km2 (rank 110 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 31 N, 36 E. Population: about 11.3 million (2026). Highest point: Jabal Umm ad Dami (1854 m). National anthem: The Royal Anthem of Jordan. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Lowest point: Dead Sea. Internet domain: .jo. Calling code: +962. Flag: The national flag of Jordan, officially adopted on 16 April 1928, is based on the 1916 flag of the Arab Revolt against the Ottoman Empire during World War I. The flag consists of horizontal black, white, and green bands, with a red isoceles triangle based at the hoist. The colors are the pan-Arab colors, representing the Abbasid, Umayyad, and Fatimid or Rashidun caliphates. The red triangle represents the Hashemite dynasty, and the Arab Revolt.",
  JPN: "Capital: Tokyo. Currency: Japanese yen (JPY). Languages listed as official or national: Japanese. People are called: Japanese. Area: 377,930 km2 (rank 61 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 36 N, 138 E. Population: about 123.8 million (2024). Highest point: Mount Fuji (3777 m). National anthem: Kimigayo. Official languages and their families: Japanese (language families: Japonic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Hachirōgata. Internet domain: .jp. Calling code: +81. Flag: The national flag of Japan is a rectangular white banner with a red circle at its center. The flag is officially called the Nisshōki , but is more commonly known in Japan as the Hinomaru . It embodies the country's sobriquet: the Land of the Rising Sun.",
  KAZ: "Capital: Astana. Currency: Kazakhstani tenge (KZT). Languages listed as official or national: Kazakh, Russian. People are called: Kazakhstani. Area: 2,724,900 km2 (rank 9 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 48 N, 68 E. Population: about 20.1 million (2024). Highest point: Khan Tengri (7010 m). National anthem: Menıñ Qazaqstanym. Official languages and their families: Kazakh (language families: Kipchak–Nogai, Kipchak, Common Turkic languages, Turkic); Russian (language families: East Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Karagiye. Internet domain: .kz. Calling code: +7. Flag: The State Flag of the Republic of Kazakhstan is a turquoise banner consisting of a 32-ray gold sun above a soaring golden steppe eagle, with the hoist side displaying a national ornamental pattern 'qoşqar-müiız'. It was adopted on 4 June 1992, designed by Shaken Niyazbekov, and replacing the Soviet-era flag.",
  KEN: "Capital: Nairobi. Currency: Kenyan shilling (KES). Languages listed as official or national: English, Swahili. People are called: Kenyan. Area: 580,367 km2 (rank 47 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1 N, 38 E. Highest point: Mount Kenya (5199 m). National anthem: Ee Mungu Nguvu Yetu. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Swahili (language families: Bantu, Sabaki, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Northeast Coast Bantu, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Northeast Bantu, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, East Bantu). Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .ke. Calling code: +254. Flag: The flag of Kenya is a tricolour of black, red, and green with two white edges imposed with a red, white and black Maasai shield and two crossed spears. The flag is mainly based on that of Kenya African National Union and was officially adopted on 12 December 1963 upon Kenya's independence.",
  KGZ: "Capital: Bishkek. Currency: Kyrgyzstani som (KGS). Languages listed as official or national: Kyrgyz, Russian. People are called: Kirghiz. Area: 199,951 km2 (rank 85 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 41 N, 75 E. Population: about 7.3 million (2025). Highest point: Jengish Chokusu (7439 m). National anthem: State Anthem of the Kyrgyz Republic. Official languages and their families: Kyrgyz (language families: Kyrgyz–Kipchak, Kipchak, Common Turkic languages, Turkic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Kara Darya. Internet domain: .kg. Calling code: +996. Flag: The State Flag of the Kyrgyz Republic consists of a red field charged with a yellow sun that contains a depiction of a tündük, the opening in the center of the roof of a yurt. Adopted in 1992, just over seven months after the country's independence was declared, to replace the flag of the Kirghiz SSR, it has been the flag of Kyrgyzstan since that year. The red on the flag is said to be inspired by the pennant lifted by Manas, the country's folk hero.",
  KHM: "Capital: Phnom Penh. Currency: Cambodian riel (KHR), United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: Khmer. People are called: Cambodian. Area: 181,035 km2 (rank 88 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13 N, 105 E. Population: about 17.4 million (2023). Highest point: Phnom Aural (1813 m). National anthem: Nokor Reach. Official languages and their families: Khmer (language families: Mon-Khmer languages, Eastern Mon-Khmer, Austroasiatic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Gulf of Thailand. Internet domain: .kh. Calling code: +855. Flag: The flag of Cambodia features three horizontal bands of blue, double-width red, and blue, with a white depiction of Angkor Wat centred on the red band. Red and blue are traditionally the colours of Cambodia, representing the nation and the king respectively.",
  KIR: "Capital: South Tarawa. Currency: Australian dollar (AUD), Kiribati dollar (KID). Languages listed as official or national: English, Gilbertese. People are called: I-Kiribati. Area: 811 km2 (rank 172 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1.41666666 N, 173 E. Population: about 0.1 million (2024). Highest point: Banaba Island (81 m). National anthem: Kunan Kiribati. Official languages and their families: Gilbertese (language families: Micronesian, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian); English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .ki. Calling code: +686. Flag: The flag of Kiribati is red in the upper half with a gold frigatebird flying over a gold rising sun (otintaai), and the lower half is blue with three horizontal wavy white stripes to represent the ocean and the three archipelagoes. The 17 rays of the sun represent the 16 Gilbert Islands and Banaba.",
  KNA: "Capital: Basseterre. Currency: Eastern Caribbean dollar (XCD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Kittitian or Nevisian. Area: 261 km2 (rank 188 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 17.33333333 N, 62.75 W. Population: about 0.0 million (2025). Highest point: Mount Liamuiga (1156 m). National anthem: O Land of Beauty!. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, federal monarchy. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .kn. Calling code: +1869. Flag: The national flag of Saint Kitts and Nevis consists of a yellow-edged black band containing two white stars that divides diagonally from the lower hoist-side corner, with a green upper triangle and red lower triangle. Adopted in 1983 to replace the flag of Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla, it has been the flag of the Federation of Saint Kitts and Nevis since the country gained independence that year. Although the flag utilises the colours of the Pan-Africanist movement, the symbolism behind them is interpreted differently.",
  KOR: "Capital: Seoul. Currency: South Korean won (KRW). Languages listed as official or national: Korean. People are called: South Korean. Area: 100,210 km2 (rank 107 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 37 N, 127.5 E. Population: about 51.6 million (2022). Highest point: Hallasan (1947 m). National anthem: Aegukga. Official languages and their families: Korean (language families: Koreanic, Altaic); Korean Sign Language. Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Sea of Japan. Internet domain: .kr. Calling code: +82. Flag: The national flag of the Republic of Korea, also known as the Taegeukgi, consists of three components: a white rectangular background, a red and blue taegeuk in its center, accompanied by four black trigrams, one in each corner. The predecessors to the current Taegeukgi were used as the national flag of Korea by the Joseon dynasty, the Korean Empire, as well as the Korean government-in-exile during Japanese rule. South Korea adopted Taegeukgi for its national flag in 1948.",
  KWT: "Capital: Kuwait City. Currency: Kuwaiti dinar (KWD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Kuwaiti. Area: 17,818 km2 (rank 152 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 29.5 N, 45.75 E. Population: about 4.3 million (2026). Highest point: Mutla Ridge (140 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Kuwait. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Persian Gulf. Internet domain: .kw. Calling code: +965. Flag: The flag of Kuwait was adopted on 7 September 1961, and officially hoisted 24 November 1961. Before 1961, the flag of Kuwait was red and white, like those of other Arab states of the Persian Gulf at the time, with the field being red and words or charges being written in white. It is the only national flag featuring an acute trapezium.",
  LAO: "Capital: Vientiane. Currency: Lao kip (LAK). Languages listed as official or national: Lao. People are called: Laotian. Area: 236,800 km2 (rank 82 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 18 N, 105 E. Highest point: Phou Bia (2819 m). National anthem: Pheng Xat Lao. Official languages and their families: Lao (language families: Southwestern Tai, Tai, Kam–Tai, Kra–Dai, Austro-Tai). Form of government: people's republic, communist dictatorship. Lowest point: Mekong River. Internet domain: .la. Calling code: +856. Flag: The national flag of the Lao People's Democratic Republic consists of three horizontal stripes, with the middle stripe in blue being twice the height of the top and bottom red stripes. In the middle is a white disc, the diameter of the disc is 4⁄5 the height of the blue stripe. The flag ratio is 2:3. The flag was first adopted in 1945 under the Lao Issara government of 1945–46, then by the Pathet Lao.",
  LBN: "Capital: Beirut. Currency: Lebanese pound (LBP). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, French. People are called: Lebanese. Area: 10,452 km2 (rank 161 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 33.83333333 N, 35.83333333 E. Population: about 5.3 million (2026). Highest point: Qurnat as Sawda' (3088 m). National anthem: Lebanese national anthem. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Mediterranean Sea. Internet domain: .lb. Calling code: +961. Flag: The national flag of Lebanon is a horizontal triband of two red stripes enveloping a central white stripe which is twice the height of each red stripe. Centered on the white stripe is a green cedar of Lebanon tree, touching both red stripes. The flag was adopted on 7 December 1943, shortly before Lebanon gained independence from France. The flag's origins trace back to the early 20th century, with the cedar tree becoming a prominent symbol during the French mandate period. The current design was finalized during a tense political climate in 1943, when Lebanon unilaterally abolished the French mandate. The flag was formally introduced into the Lebanese Constitution on 7 December 1943, with its design standardized in 1990 to feature an entirely green cedar tree. In 1979, 21 November was declared as National Flag Day to honor the first flag-raising in 1943.",
  LBR: "Capital: Monrovia. Currency: Liberian dollar (LRD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Liberian. Area: 111,369 km2 (rank 102 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 6.5 N, 9.5 W. Highest point: Mount Wuteve (1440 m). National anthem: All Hail, Liberia, Hail!. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: republic, presidential system, constitutional republic, Herrenvolk democracy. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .lr. Calling code: +231. Flag: The flag of Liberia, occasionally referred to as the Lone Star, bears a close resemblance to the flag of the United States, representing Liberia's founding by free people of color and former slaves from the United States and the Caribbean. They are both part of the stars and stripes flag family.",
  LBY: "Capital: Tripoli. Currency: Libyan dinar (LYD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Libyan. Area: 1,759,540 km2 (rank 16 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 25 N, 17 E. Population: about 7.1 million (2026). Highest point: Bikku Bitti (2267 m). National anthem: Libya, Libya, Libya. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Sabkhat Ghuzayyil. Internet domain: .ly. Calling code: +218. Flag: The national flag of Libya was originally introduced in 1951, following the creation of the Kingdom of Libya. It was designed by Omar Faiek Shennib and approved by King Idris Al Senussi who comprised the UN delegation representing the three regions of Cyrenaica, Fezzan, and Tripolitania at UN unification discussions.",
  LCA: "Capital: Castries. Currency: Eastern Caribbean dollar (XCD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Saint Lucian. Area: 616 km2 (rank 178 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.88333333 N, 60.96666666 W. Population: about 0.2 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Gimie (952 m). National anthem: Sons and Daughters of Saint Lucia. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .lc. Calling code: +1758. Flag: The national flag of Saint Lucia consists of a cerulean blue field charged with a golden triangle in front of a white-edged black isosceles triangle. Adopted in 1967 to replace the British Blue Ensign defaced with the arms of the colony, it has been the flag of Saint Lucia since the country became an associated state of the United Kingdom that year. Although the overall design of the flag has remained unchanged, specific aspects of it have been altered over the years.",
  LIE: "Capital: Vaduz. Currency: Swiss franc (CHF). Languages listed as official or national: German. People are called: Liechtensteiner. Area: 160 km2 (rank 190 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 47.26666666 N, 9.53333333 E. Highest point: Grauspitz (2599 m). National anthem: Oben am jungen Rhein. Official languages and their families: German (language families: West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Rhine. Internet domain: .li. Calling code: +423. Flag: The national flag of the Principality of Liechtenstein consists of two horizontal bands, one blue and one red, charged with a gold crown in the canton. In use since 1852 and officially enshrined into the nation's constitution in 1921, it has been the flag of the principality since that year. The crown was added to the flag in 1937, after the country discovered at the Summer Olympics held the previous year that their flag was identical to the Haitian civil flag. A design with a modified crown was adopted on 30 June 1982.",
  LKA: "Capital: Colombo. Currency: Sri Lankan rupee (LKR). Languages listed as official or national: Sinhala, Tamil. People are called: Sri Lankan. Area: 65,610 km2 (rank 120 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 7 N, 81 E. Population: about 22.0 million (2023). Highest point: Pidurutalagala (2524 m). National anthem: Sri Lanka Matha. Official languages and their families: Sinhala (language families: Insular Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian, Indo-Aryan, Indo-European); Tamil (language families: Tamil languages, Tamil–Malayalam, Tamil–Kannada, South Dravidian, Dravidian). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .lk. Calling code: +94. Flag: The national flag of Sri Lanka, also called the Singha Flag or Lion Flag, consists of a golden lion holding a kastane sword in its right fore-paw in a maroon background with four gold bo leaves, one in each corner. This is bordered by gold, and to its left are two vertical stripes of equal size in teal and orange, with the orange stripe closest to the lion.",
  LSO: "Capital: Maseru. Currency: Lesotho loti (LSL), South African rand (ZAR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Sotho. People are called: Mosotho. Area: 30,355 km2 (rank 137 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 29.5 S, 28.5 E. Highest point: Thabana Ntlenyana (3482 m). National anthem: Lesotho Fatse La Bontata Rona. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Sesotho (language families: Sotho–Tswana, Bantu, Southern Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Orange River. Internet domain: .ls. Calling code: +266. Flag: The flag of Lesotho, adopted on the 40th anniversary of Lesotho's independence on 4 October 2006, features a horizontal blue, white, and green tricolour with a black mokorotlo in the center. The design is intended to reflect a state that is both at peace internally and with its only neighbour South Africa, replacing the old flag design that featured a military emblem of a shield, spear and knobkerrie.",
  LTU: "Capital: Vilnius. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Lithuanian. People are called: Lithuanian. Area: 65,300 km2 (rank 121 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 56 N, 24 E. Population: about 2.9 million (2023). Highest point: Aukštojas Hill (294 m). National anthem: Tautiška giesmė. Official languages and their families: Lithuanian (language families: East Baltic, Baltic, Balto-Slavic, Indo-European). Form of government: semi-presidential system, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Nemunas Delta. Internet domain: .lt. Calling code: +370. Flag: The national flag of Lithuania consists of a horizontal tricolour of yellow, green, and red. It was adopted on 25 April 1918 during Lithuania's first period of independence (1918–1940), which ceased with the occupation first by the Soviet Union, and then by Nazi Germany (1941–1944). During the post-World War II Soviet occupation, from 1945 until 1988, the Flag of the Lithuanian SSR consisted first of a generic red Soviet flag with the name of the republic, in 1953 that was changed to the red flag with white and green bands at the bottom.",
  LUX: "Capital: Luxembourg. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: German, French, Luxembourgish. People are called: Luxembourger. Area: 2,586 km2 (rank 168 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 49.75 N, 6.16666666 E. Population: about 0.7 million (2026). Highest point: Kneiff (560 m). National anthem: Ons Heemecht. Official languages and their families: Luxembourgish (language families: Franconian, West Central German, Central German, Weser-Rhine Germanic, Old Germanic, West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Germanic languages, Indo-European, Northwest Germanic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); German (language families: West Germanic languages, High German, South Germanic, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Moselle. Internet domain: .lu. Calling code: +352. Flag: The national flag of Luxembourg is a tricolour flag, that consists of three horizontal stripes; watermelon red, white and light blue, and can be in 2:1 or 5:3 ratio. It was first used between 1845 and 1848 and officially adopted in 1993. It is informally called in the country: «Rout-Wäiss-Blo» ('Red-White-Blue').",
  LVA: "Capital: Riga. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Latvian. People are called: Latvian. Area: 64,559 km2 (rank 122 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 57 N, 25 E. Population: about 1.8 million (2026). Highest point: Gaiziņkalns (312 m). National anthem: Dievs, svētī Latviju!. Official languages and their families: Latvian (language families: East Baltic, Baltic, Balto-Slavic, Indo-European). Form of government: republic, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Baltic Sea. Internet domain: .lv. Calling code: +371. Flag: The national flag of Latvia is a triband featuring two wide horizontal stripes of red at the top and bottom, separated by a twice narrower white centre stripe. It was used as the national flag after Latvia became an independent country in 1918. After the Soviet Union invaded and occupied Latvia in 1940, its use was suppressed by the Soviet government. On 27 February 1990, shortly before Latvia regained independence, the government re-adopted the traditional red-white-red flag.",
  MAR: "Capital: Rabat. Currency: Moroccan dirham (MAD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, Berber. People are called: Moroccan. Area: 446,550 km2 (rank 57 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 32 N, 5 W. Population: about 38.3 million (2026). Highest point: Jbel Toubkal (4167 m). National anthem: Cherifian Anthem. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); Standard Moroccan Tamazight (language families: Atlas (Amazigh), Northern Berber languages, Afroasiatic, Berber). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Sebkha Tah. Internet domain: .ma. Calling code: +212. Flag: The flag of Morocco (French: Drapeau de Maroc) is the flag used by the government of Morocco and has served as the national flag of Morocco since 17 November 1915. It has a red field with a green pentagram in the center. The green star represents the five pillars of Islam, and the red represents the blood of the ancestors and unity.",
  MCO: "Capital: Monaco. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Monegasque. Area: 2 km2 (rank 194 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 43.73333333 N, 7.4 E. Population: about 0.0 million (2025). Highest point: Chemin des Révoires (162 m). National anthem: Hymne monégasque. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, hereditary monarchy. Lowest point: Mediterranean Sea. Internet domain: .mc. Calling code: +377. Flag: The national flag of Monaco has two equal horizontal bands, of red (top) and white (bottom), both of which have been the heraldic colours of the House of Grimaldi since at least 1339. The present bicolour design was adopted on 4 April 1881, under Charles III.",
  MDA: "Capital: Chișinău. Currency: Moldovan leu (MDL). Languages listed as official or national: Moldavian. People are called: Moldovan. Area: 33,846 km2 (rank 135 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 47 N, 29 E. Population: about 2.4 million (2024). Highest point: Bălănești Hill (430 m). National anthem: Limba noastră. Official languages and their families: Romanian (language families: Eastern Romance, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: unitary state, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Dniester. Internet domain: .md. Calling code: +373. Flag: The national flag of the Republic of Moldova is a vertical triband of blue, yellow, and red, charged with the coat of arms of Moldova on the centre bar. The reverse is mirrored. The flag ratio is 1:2. Until further provisions, the State Flag of Moldova is used as the national flag and ensign as well; that is, civil, state and war flag and ensign.",
  MDG: "Capital: Antananarivo. Currency: Malagasy ariary (MGA). Languages listed as official or national: French, Malagasy. People are called: Malagasy. Area: 587,041 km2 (rank 45 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 20 S, 47 E. Population: about 32.0 million (2024). Highest point: Maromokotro (2876 m). National anthem: Ry Tanindrazanay malala ô!. Official languages and their families: Malagasy (language families: East Barito, Barito, Malayo-Polynesian, Dayak, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .mg. Calling code: +261. Flag: The national flag of Madagascar is a tricolour featuring two equal horizontal bands of red and green with a vertical white band of the same size on the hoist side. It was designed by Andrianome Ranaivosoa, an agent of the Malagasy National Geographic Institute. The design was chosen by a technical commission on 15 October 1958 and officially adopted on 21 October, one day and one week after the Malagasy Republic was proclaimed, respectively.",
  MDV: "Capital: Malé. Currency: Maldivian rufiyaa (MVR). Languages listed as official or national: Maldivian. People are called: Maldivan. Area: 300 km2 (rank 187 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 3.25 N, 73 E. Highest point: Villingili (5 m). National anthem: Gaumiii salaaam. Official languages and their families: Maldivian (language families: Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .mv. Calling code: +960. Flag: The Flag of the Republic of Maldives is green with a red border. The center bears a vertical white crescent; the closed side of the crescent is on the raising side of the flag. It was adopted on 25 July 1965.",
  MEX: "Capital: Mexico City. Currency: Mexican peso (MXN). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Mexican. Area: 1,964,375 km2 (rank 13 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 23 N, 102 W. Population: about 132.3 million (2024). Highest point: Citlaltepetl (5636 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Mexico. Official languages and their families: Yucatec Maya (language families: Mayan, Central American Indian languages, Yucatecan); Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Nahuatl (language families: Uto-Aztecan, Nahuan languages). Form of government: empire, presidential system, federal republic, constitutional republic, democratic republic. Lowest point: Laguna Salada. Internet domain: .mx. Calling code: +52. Flag: The national flag of Mexico is a vertical tricolor of green, white, and red with the national coat of arms charged in the center of the white stripe. While the meaning of the colors has changed over time, these three colors were adopted by Mexico following independence from Spain during the country's War of Independence, and subsequent First Mexican Empire.",
  MHL: "Capital: Majuro. Currency: United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Marshallese. People are called: Marshallese. Area: 181 km2 (rank 189 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 9 N, 168 E. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: Likiep Atoll (10 m). National anthem: Forever Marshall Islands. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Marshallese (language families: Oceanic, Micronesian, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .mh. Calling code: +692. Flag: The flag of the Republic of the Marshall Islands, an island nation in the Pacific, was adopted upon the start of self-government, May 1, 1979. The flag was designed by Emlain Kabua, who served as the first First Lady of the republic.",
  MKD: "Capital: Skopje. Currency: denar (MKD). Languages listed as official or national: Macedonian. People are called: Macedonian. Area: 25,713 km2 (rank 145 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 41.83333333 N, 22 E. Highest point: Korab (2764 m). National anthem: Denes nad Makedonija. Official languages and their families: Macedonian (language families: South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic); Albanian (language families: Indo-European). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Vardar. Internet domain: .mk. Calling code: +389. Flag: The national flag of North Macedonia depicts a stylized yellow sun on a red field, with eight broadening rays extending from the center to the edge of the field. It was designed by Miroslav Grčev and was adopted on 5 October 1995.",
  MLI: "Capital: Bamako. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Malian. Area: 1,240,192 km2 (rank 23 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 17 N, 4 W. Population: about 22.4 million (2022). Highest point: Mount Hombori (1153 m). National anthem: Pour l'Afrique et pour toi. Official languages and their families: Bobo (language families: Northwestern Mande, Western Mande, Mande, Niger–Congo); Tamasheq (language families: Berber, Afroasiatic); Soninke (language families: Mande, Niger–Congo); Bozo (language families: Mande, Niger–Congo); Fula (language families: Senegambian, Atlantic, Atlantic–Congo, Niger–Congo); Senufo (language families: Gur, Niger–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo); Bambara (language families: Mande, Niger–Congo); Hassaniya Arabic (language families: Maghrebi Arabic, Semitic, Afroasiatic); Kassonke (language families: Mande, Manding, Niger–Congo); Maninka (language families: Niger–Congo, Manding, Mande); Songhay (language families: Nilo-Saharan); Dogon (language families: Benue–Congo, Niger–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo); Minyanka (language families: Niger–Congo, Volta–Congo, Senufo, Gur, Atlantic–Congo). Lowest point: Senegal River. Internet domain: .ml. Calling code: +223. Flag: The national flag of Mali is a vertical tricolour of green, gold, and red, the pan-African colours.",
  MLT: "Capital: Valletta. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Maltese. People are called: Maltese. Area: 316 km2 (rank 186 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 35.83333333 N, 14.58333333 E. Population: about 0.6 million (2023). Highest point: Ta' Dmejrek (253 m). National anthem: L-Innu Malti. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Maltese (language families: Maghrebi Arabic, Semitic, Afroasiatic). Form of government: parliamentary democracy. Lowest point: Mediterranean Sea. Internet domain: .mt. Calling code: +356. Flag: The flag of Malta is a bicolour, with white in the hoist and red in the fly. A representation of the George Cross, awarded to Malta by George VI in 1942, is carried, edged with red, in the canton of the white stripe. The flag was first recognized in May of 1952. It is the only national flag to feature English-language text. It is one of two national flags, alongside Belize, to depict a human.",
  MMR: "Capital: Naypyidaw. Currency: Burmese kyat (MMK). Languages listed as official or national: Burmese. People are called: Burmese. Area: 676,578 km2 (rank 39 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 22 N, 98 E. Highest point: Hkakabo Razi (5881 m). National anthem: Kaba Ma Kyei. Official languages and their families: Burmese (language families: Sino-Tibetan, Dené–Caucasian, Sino-Austronesian, Sino-Tibetan-Indo-European). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .mm. Calling code: +95. Flag: The State Flag of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar is a horizontal rectangular tricolor flag of yellow, green, and red with a large white five-pointed star in the center. The current flag was adopted on 21 October 2010.",
  MNE: "Capital: Podgorica. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Montenegrin. People are called: Montenegrin. Area: 13,812 km2 (rank 156 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 42.5 N, 19.3 E. Population: about 0.6 million (2022). Highest point: Zla Kolata (2534 m). National anthem: Oj, svijetla majska zoro. Official languages and their families: Montenegrin (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Adriatic Sea. Internet domain: .me. Calling code: +382. Flag: The national flag of Montenegro has a red field with gold border and the coat of arms of Montenegro in its center. It was officially adopted on 13 July 2004, when the then Republic of Montenegro was a constituent of the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro, and its precise specification was standardized on 16 September 2004. The flag was retained after Montenegrin independence in 2006, and mandated by Article 4 of the Constitution of Montenegro adopted in 2007.",
  MNG: "Capital: Ulan Bator. Currency: Mongolian tögrög (MNT). Languages listed as official or national: Mongolian. People are called: Mongolian. Area: 1,564,110 km2 (rank 18 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 46 N, 105 E. Highest point: Hüiten Peak (4374 m). National anthem: National anthem of Mongolia. Official languages and their families: Mongolian (language families: Mongolic). Form of government: parliamentary system. Lowest point: Hoh Nuur. Internet domain: .mn. Calling code: +976. Flag: The national flag of Mongolia is a vertical triband with a red stripe at each side and a blue stripe in the middle, with the Mongolian Soyombo symbol centred on the stripe nearest the hoist. The blue stripe represents the eternal blue sky, the red stripes thriving for eternity, and the yellow color symbolize Tibetan Buddhism. The Soyombo symbol is a geometric abstraction that represents fire, sun, moon, earth, water, and a Yin and Yang symbol representing two fish as in Mongol mythology fish never sleep thus symbolizing that the spirit of the Mongol people never sleeps.",
  MOZ: "Capital: Maputo. Currency: Mozambican metical (MZN). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese. People are called: Mozambican. Area: 801,590 km2 (rank 35 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 18.25 S, 35 E. Highest point: Monte Binga (2436 m). National anthem: Pátria Amada. Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Lowest point: Mozambique Channel. Internet domain: .mz. Calling code: +258. Flag: The national flag of Mozambique is a horizontal tricolour of green, black, and golden-yellow with white fimbriations and a red isosceles triangle at the hoist. The triangle is charged with a five-pointed gold star in its centre, above which there is a bayonet-equipped AK-47 crossed by a hoe, superimposed on an open book. The colours and symbols of the flag represent different aspects of the Mozambican people and their war of independence.",
  MRT: "Capital: Nouakchott. Currency: Mauritanian ouguiya (MRU). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Mauritanian. Area: 1,030,700 km2 (rank 28 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 20 N, 12 W. Population: about 4.9 million (2026). Highest point: Kediet ej Jill (915 m). National anthem: National anthem of Mauritania. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Sebkha de Ndrhamcha. Internet domain: .mr. Calling code: +222. Flag: The national flag of Mauritania is a green field bordered above and below by a horizontal red band and charged in the centre with an upward-pointing golden star and crescent. It is a derivative of the country's first flag, which omitted the red bands. The current flag was adopted by decree in 2017 following a constitutional referendum in which 86% of voters voted in favour of changing the flag. However, the design was not standardised until 2020, when the government of Mauritania published official graphic guidelines for the national flag.",
  MUS: "Capital: Port Louis. Currency: Mauritian rupee (MUR). Languages listed as official or national: English, French, Mauritian Creole. People are called: Mauritian. Area: 2,040 km2 (rank 169 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 20.28333333 S, 57.55 E. Highest point: Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire (828 m). National anthem: Motherland. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .mu. Calling code: +230. Flag: The national flag of Mauritius, also known as The Four Bands, was adopted upon independence, 12 March 1968. It consists of four horizontal bands of equal width, coloured red, blue, yellow, and green. The flag was recorded at the College of Arms in London on 9 January 1968.",
  MWI: "Capital: Lilongwe. Currency: Malawian kwacha (MWK). Languages listed as official or national: English, Chewa. People are called: Malawian. Area: 118,484 km2 (rank 98 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.5 S, 34 E. Highest point: Mulanje Massif (3002 m). National anthem: Mulungu dalitsa Malaŵi. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Tumbuka (language families: Nyasa, East Bantu, Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Chewa (language families: Nyasa, East Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo). Lowest point: Shire River. Internet domain: .mw. Calling code: +265. Flag: The national flag of Malawi was officially adopted on 6 July 1964 when the colony of Nyasaland became independent from British rule and renamed itself Malawi.",
  MYS: "Capital: Kuala Lumpur. Currency: Malaysian ringgit (MYR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Malay. People are called: Malaysian. Area: 330,803 km2 (rank 67 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 2.5 N, 112.5 E. Population: about 34.2 million (2025). Highest point: Mount Kinabalu (4095 m). National anthem: Negaraku. Official languages and their families: Malay (language families: Malayan, Malayic, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, federation, elective monarchy, federal monarchy, parliamentary monarchy. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .my. Calling code: +60. Flag: The national flag of Malaysia, also known as the Stripes of Glory, is composed of a field of 14 alternating red and white stripes along the fly, and a blue canton bearing a crescent and a 14-point star known as the Bintang Persekutuan.",
  NAM: "Capital: Windhoek. Currency: Namibian dollar (NAD), South African rand (ZAR). Languages listed as official or national: Afrikaans, German, English, Herero, Khoekhoe, Kwangali, Lozi, Ndonga, Tswana. People are called: Namibian. Area: 825,615 km2 (rank 34 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 22 S, 17 E. Highest point: Brandberg (2573 m). National anthem: Namibia, Land of the Brave. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .na. Calling code: +264. Flag: The flag of Namibia consists of a red bend sinister fimbriated white, separating a blue upper triangle charged with a twelve-rayed yellow sun from a green lower triangle.",
  NER: "Capital: Niamey. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Nigerien. Area: 1,267,000 km2 (rank 21 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 16 N, 8 E. Highest point: Mont Idoukal-n-Taghès (2022 m). National anthem: L'Honneur de la Patrie. Official languages and their families: Hausa (language families: West Chadic, Hausa–Gwandara, West Chadic A, Chadic, Afroasiatic). Lowest point: Niger River. Internet domain: .ne. Calling code: +227. Flag: The flag of Niger has been the national flag since 1959, a year prior to its formal independence from French West Africa. It uses the national colors of orange, white and green, in equal horizontal bands, with an orange circle in the center. It forms one of the official national symbols of the Republic of Niger, along with the coat of arms, the national anthem, and the national motto: 'Fraternité, Travail, Progrès'.",
  NGA: "Capital: Abuja. Currency: Nigerian naira (NGN). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Nigerian. Area: 923,768 km2 (rank 31 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 10 N, 8 E. Highest point: Chappal Waddi (2419 m). National anthem: Nigeria, We Hail Thee. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .ng. Calling code: +234. Flag: The flag of Nigeria was designed by Taiwo Akinkunmi and was officially adopted to represent Nigeria at midnight on 1 October 1960, the day the country gained independence from the United Kingdom. The flag was chosen as part of a nationwide open contest held by the government, with Akinkunmi's design being selected as the winner of a field of over three thousand entries. The flag is a vertical bicolour green-white-green design, with green representing agriculture and white representing peace and unity.",
  NIC: "Capital: Managua. Currency: Nicaraguan córdoba (NIO). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Nicaraguan. Area: 130,373 km2 (rank 96 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13 N, 85 W. Population: about 7.0 million (2025). Highest point: Cerro Mogotón (2107 m). National anthem: Salve a ti. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .ni. Calling code: +505. Flag: The flag of Nicaragua was first adopted on September 11th, 1908, but not made official until August 27, 1971. It is based on, and inspired by, the flag of the Federal Republic of Central America, with a blue and white triband and a coat of arms as the charge in the centre. Its flag is one of the few that currently use the color purple, due to the rainbow in its coat of arms.",
  NLD: "Capital: Amsterdam. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Dutch. People are called: Dutch. Area: 41,850 km2 (rank 131 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 52.5 N, 5.75 E. Highest point: Mount Scenery (870 m). National anthem: Wilhelmus. Official languages and their families: Dutch (language families: Low Franconian, Franconian, West Germanic languages, High German, West Central German, Weser-Rhine Germanic, South Germanic, Old Germanic, North Sea Germanic, Central German, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Zuidplaspolder. Internet domain: .nl. Calling code: +31. Flag: The national flag of the Netherlands is a horizontal tricolour of red, white, and blue. The current design originates as a variant of the late 16th century orange-white-blue Prinsenvlag, evolving in the early 17th century as the red-white-blue Statenvlag, the naval flag of the States-General of the Dutch Republic, making the Dutch flag the oldest tricolour flag in continuous use. As a flag that symbolises the transformation from monarchy to republic, it has inspired both the derivative Russian flag, and after the French Revolution in 1789, the vertically striped French tricolour; both flags in turn influenced many other tricolours. During the 1920s and the economic crisis of the 1930s, the old Prince's Flag with the colour orange gained some popularity among Protestants, Orangists, conservative liberals, fascists, and others. To end the confusion, the colours red, white and blue and its official status as the national flag of the Kingdom of the Netherlands were reaffirmed by royal decree on 19 February 1937.",
  NOR: "Capital: Oslo. Currency: Norwegian krone (NOK). Languages listed as official or national: Norwegian Nynorsk, Norwegian Bokmål, Sami. People are called: Norwegian. Area: 323,802 km2 (rank 68 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 62 N, 10 E. Population: about 5.6 million (2026). Highest point: Galdhøpiggen (2469 m). National anthem: Ja, vi elsker dette landet. Official languages and their families: Nynorsk (language families: North Germanic, West Scandinavian, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Norwegian (language families: North Germanic, West Scandinavian, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Sámi (language families: Finno-Permic, Finno-Ugric, Finno-Samic, Uralic, Finno-Volgaic); Bokmål (language families: North Germanic, West Scandinavian, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, parliamentary system, unitary state, representative democracy. Lowest point: Norwegian Sea. Internet domain: .no. Calling code: +47. Flag: The national flag of Norway is red with a navy blue Scandinavian cross bordered in white that extends to the edges of the flag; the vertical part of the cross is shifted to the hoist side in the style of the Dannebrog, the flag of Denmark.",
  NPL: "Capital: Kathmandu. Currency: Nepalese rupee (NPR). Languages listed as official or national: Nepali. People are called: Nepalese. Area: 147,181 km2 (rank 93 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 28 N, 84 E. Highest point: Mount Everest (8849 m). National anthem: Sayaun Thunga Phulka. Official languages and their families: Nepali (language families: Indo-Aryan, Northern Indo-Aryan, Eastern Pahari, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Form of government: people's republic, federal republic. Lowest point: Kechana Kawal. Internet domain: .np. Calling code: +977. Flag: The national flag of Nepal is a concave pentagonal flag of red, white, and blue colour. It is used as both the state and civil flag of Nepal. It is the only non-rectangular national flag in the world and the only one that is taller than it is wide. The flag's unique shape is a combination of two single pennants, and is known as a double-pennon. The red colour of the field represents bravery and Nepal's national flower, the rhododendron, while the blue colour of the border represents peace.",
  NRU: "Capital: Yaren. Currency: Australian dollar (AUD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Nauru. People are called: Nauruan. Area: 21 km2 (rank 193 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 0.53333333 S, 166.91666666 E. Highest point: Command Ridge (71 m). National anthem: Nauru Bwiema. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Nauruan (language families: Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Micronesian, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .nr. Calling code: +674. Flag: The national flag of Nauru is a blue field with a horizontal gold stripe and a twelve pointed white star underneath the line on the left side. The blue field represents the Pacific Ocean, the gold stripe represents the Equator, and the star represents Nauru's geographic location, which is less than one degree under the Equator. It was adopted after a local competition held after the country gained independence in 1968.",
  NZL: "Capital: Wellington. Currency: New Zealand dollar (NZD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Māori, New Zealand Sign Language. People are called: New Zealander. Area: 270,467 km2 (rank 75 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 41 S, 174 E. Population: about 5.3 million (2024). Highest point: Aoraki / Mount Cook (3724 m). National anthem: God Defend New Zealand. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Māori (language families: Nuclear Polynesian, Tahitic, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Polynesian, Central Pacific, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Oceanic, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); New Zealand Sign Language. Form of government: parliamentary monarchy. Lowest point: Taieri Plain. Internet domain: .nz. Calling code: +64. Flag: The flag of New Zealand, also known as the New Zealand Ensign, is based on the British maritime Blue Ensign – a blue field with the Union Jack in the canton or upper hoist corner – augmented or defaced with four red stars centred within four white stars, representing the Southern Cross constellation.",
  OMN: "Capital: Muscat. Currency: Omani rial (OMR). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Omani. Area: 309,500 km2 (rank 71 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 21 N, 57 E. Population: about 5.1 million (2026). Highest point: Jebel Shams (3018 m). National anthem: Nashid as-Salaam as-Sultani. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: absolute monarchy. Lowest point: Majlis al Jinn. Internet domain: .om. Calling code: +968. Flag: The national flag of Oman consists of two horizontal stripes of upper white and lower green, separated by a red horizontal 'T' shape in the centre that contains the national emblem of Oman.",
  PAK: "Capital: Islamabad. Currency: Pakistani rupee (PKR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Urdu. People are called: Pakistani. Area: 881,912 km2 (rank 33 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 30 N, 70 E. Highest point: K2 (8611 m). National anthem: Qaumi Taranah. Official languages and their families: Urdu (language families: Western Hindi, Central Indo-Aryan, Indo-Aryan, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European); English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: federal republic, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Arabian Sea. Internet domain: .pk. Calling code: +92. Flag: The national flag of Pakistan, often referred to as the Flag of the Star and Crescent, is made up of a green field with a stylized tilted white descending crescent moon and five-pointed star at its centre, and a vertical white stripe at its hoist-end. The flag has become the predominant and most recognizable symbol of Pakistan. Though the specific shade of green on the flag is mandated only as 'dark green', its official and most consistent representation is in Pakistan green, which is shaded distinctively darker and is represented by the Pantone color 19-5917 TCX. It was adopted by the Constituent Assembly of Pakistan on 11 August 1947, and it became the official flag of the Dominion of Pakistan on 14 August 1947, following independence from the British Empire. The flag was subsequently retained as that of the Islamic Republic of Pakistan in 1956 and remains in use as the national flag to this day.",
  PAN: "Capital: Panama City. Currency: Panamanian balboa (PAB), United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Panamanian. Area: 75,417 km2 (rank 116 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 9 N, 80 W. Population: about 4.6 million (2025). Highest point: Volcan Baru (3475 m). National anthem: National anthem of Panama. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .pa. Calling code: +507. Flag: The national flag of Panama was made by María de la Ossa de Amador and was officially adopted by the 'ley 48 de 1925'. The Panamanian flag day is celebrated on November 4, one day after the Panamanian separation from Colombia, and is one of a series of holidays celebrated in November known as the Fiestas Patrias.",
  PER: "Capital: Lima. Currency: Peruvian sol (PEN). Languages listed as official or national: Aymara, Quechua, Spanish. People are called: Peruvian. Area: 1,285,216 km2 (rank 19 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 10 S, 76 W. Population: about 34.2 million (2025). Highest point: Huascarán (6768 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Peru. Official languages and their families: Quechua (language families: Quechuan); Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Aymara (language families: Aymaran). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Depresión de Sechura. Internet domain: .pe. Calling code: +51. Flag: The national flag of Peru, officially named the Bandera Nacional and often referred to as The Bicolour, is a vertical triband with red outer bands and a single white middle band, as defined by Article 49 of the Constitution of the Republic of Peru. The current flag was adopted by the Congress of Peru on 25 February 1825, and modified in 1950. Depending on its use, it may be defaced with different emblems, and has different names. Flag Day in Peru is celebrated on 7 June, the anniversary of the Battle of Arica.",
  PHL: "Capital: Manila. Currency: Philippine peso (PHP). Languages listed as official or national: English, Filipino. People are called: Filipino. Area: 342,353 km2 (rank 63 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13 N, 122 E. Population: about 112.7 million (2024). Highest point: Mount Apo (2954 m). National anthem: Lupang Hinirang. Official languages and their families: Filipino (language families: Tagalogic, Central Philippine, Greater Central Philippine, Philippine, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); Taglish; English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Philippine Sea. Internet domain: .ph. Calling code: +63. Flag: The national flag of the Philippines, also known as Three Stars and a Sun, is a horizontal bicolor flag with equal bands of royal blue and crimson red, with a white, equilateral triangle at the hoist. In the center of the triangle is a golden-yellow sun with eight primary rays, to represent the original eight provinces that rebelled against the Spanish during the 1896 Philippine Revolution. At each vertex of the triangle is a five-pointed, golden-yellow star, each of which representing one of the country's three main island groups—Luzon, Visayas, and Mindanao. The white triangle at the hoist represents liberty, equality, and fraternity. A unique feature of this flag is its usage to indicate a state of war if it is displayed with the red side on top, which is effectively achieved by flipping the flag upside-down.",
  PLW: "Capital: Ngerulmud. Currency: United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Palauan. People are called: Palauan. Area: 459 km2 (rank 180 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 7.5 N, 134.5 E. Highest point: Mount Ngerchelchuus (242 m). National anthem: Belau rekid. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Palauan (language families: Western Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); Japanese (language families: Japonic). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .pw. Calling code: +680. Flag: The Flag of Palau was adopted on 1 January 1981, when the island group separated from the United Nations Trust Territory. As with the flags of several other Pacific island groups, light blue is the color used to represent the ocean and the nation's place within it. While this puts Palau in common with the Federated States of Micronesia and other neighboring island groups, the disc on the flag is similar to that on the flag of Japan but is off-centre like that of the flag of Bangladesh. In the case of Palau, the disk represents the moon instead of the sun. The current flag was introduced in 1981 when Palau became a republic.",
  PNG: "Capital: Port Moresby. Currency: Papua New Guinean kina (PGK). Languages listed as official or national: English, Hiri Motu, Tok Pisin. People are called: Papua New Guinean. Area: 462,840 km2 (rank 54 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 6 S, 147 E. Highest point: Mount Wilhelm (4509 m). National anthem: O Arise, All You Sons. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Tok Pisin; Hiri Motu (language families: Western Oceanic, Oceanic, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian); Papua New Guinean Sign Language. Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .pg. Calling code: +675. Flag: The flag of Papua New Guinea was adopted on 1 July 1971. In the hoist, it depicts the Southern Cross; in the fly, a Raggiana bird-of-paradise is silhouetted.",
  POL: "Capital: Warsaw. Currency: Polish złoty (PLN). Languages listed as official or national: Polish. People are called: Polish. Area: 312,679 km2 (rank 70 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 52 N, 20 E. Population: about 37.6 million (2024). Highest point: Rysy (2501 m). National anthem: Poland Is Not Yet Lost. Official languages and their families: Polish (language families: Lechitic, West Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: semi-presidential system, parliamentary system, unitary state. Lowest point: Żuławy Wiślane. Internet domain: .pl. Calling code: +48. Flag: The national flag of Poland consists of two horizontal stripes of equal width, the upper one white and the lower one red. The two colours are defined in the Polish constitution as the national colours. A variant of the flag with the national coat of arms in the middle of the white fess is legally reserved for official use abroad and at sea. A similar flag with the addition of a white eagle is used as the naval ensign of Poland.",
  PRK: "Capital: Pyongyang. Currency: North Korean won (KPW). Languages listed as official or national: Korean. People are called: North Korean. Area: 120,538 km2 (rank 97 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 40 N, 127 E. Population: about 26.4 million (2023). Highest point: Baekdu Mountain (2744 m). National anthem: Aegukka. Official languages and their families: North Korean standard language (language families: Koreanic, Altaic); Korean (language families: Koreanic, Altaic). Form of government: republic, one-party state, unitary state, Juche, socialist state, family dictatorship, communist dictatorship. Lowest point: Sea of Japan. Internet domain: .kp. Calling code: +850. Flag: The national flag of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea consists of a wide horizontal red stripe bordered above and below by a thin white stripe and a broad blue stripe. The red stripe is charged near the hoist with a five-pointed red star inside a white disc. The design of the flag is defined in the North Korean constitution and regulations regarding the use and manufacture of the flag are outlined in the country's national flag law.",
  PRT: "Capital: Lisbon. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese. People are called: Portuguese. Area: 92,090 km2 (rank 109 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 39.5 N, 8 W. Highest point: Mount Pico (2351 m). Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .pt. Calling code: +351. Flag: The national flag of the Portuguese Republic, often referred to as the Portuguese flag, consists of a rectangular bicolour with a field divided into green on the hoist, and red on the fly. The version without laurels of the country’s national coat of arms stands in the middle of the Portuguese armillary sphere and shield, centered over the colour boundary at equal distance. The flag was announced in 1910, following the 5 October 1910 revolution, inspired by the colours of the Republican Party and the design of radical conspiratorial society the Carbonária.",
  PRY: "Capital: Asunción. Currency: Paraguayan guaraní (PYG). Languages listed as official or national: Guaraní, Spanish. People are called: Paraguayan. Area: 406,752 km2 (rank 59 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 23 S, 58 W. Highest point: Cerro Tres Kandú (842 m). National anthem: national anthem of Paraguay. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Guarani (language families: Tupi–Guarani, Tupian). Lowest point: Paraguay River. Internet domain: .py. Calling code: +595. Flag: The flag of Paraguay was first adopted in 1842. Its design, a red–white–blue triband, was inspired by the colours of the French Tricolour, believed to signify independence and liberty. The flag is unusual because it differs on its obverse and reverse sides: the obverse of the flag shows the national seal, and the reverse shows the seal of the treasury. It is the only national flag worldwide that has a unique design on each side. The flag consists of the same three horizontal colours as the flag of the Netherlands, which in turn was the inspiration for the French flag. It was revised in 2013 to bring the flag towards its original design. It has a ratio of 11:20.",
  PSE: "Capital: Ramallah. Currency: Egyptian pound (EGP), Israeli new shekel (ILS), Jordanian dinar (JOD). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Palestinian. Area: 6,220 km2 (rank 163 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 31.9 N, 35.2 E. Population: about 5.4 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Nabi Yunis (1026 m). National anthem: Fida'i. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: semi-presidential system, unitary state, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Dead Sea. Internet domain: .ps. Calling code: +970. Flag: The national flag of Palestine is a tricolour of three equal horizontal stripes—black, white, and green from top to bottom—overlaid by a red triangle issuing from the hoist. It displays the pan-Arab colours, which were first combined in the current style during the 1916 Arab Revolt, and represents the Palestinian people and the State of Palestine.",
  QAT: "Capital: Doha. Currency: Qatari riyal (QAR). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Qatari. Area: 11,586 km2 (rank 158 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 25.5 N, 51.25 E. Highest point: Qurayn Abu al Bawl (103 m). National anthem: As Salam al Amiri. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Persian Gulf. Internet domain: .qa. Calling code: +974. Flag: The flag of Qatar is in the ratio of 11:28. It is maroon with a broad white serrated band of nine points at the hoist. It was adopted shortly before the country declared independence from the United Kingdom on 3 September 1971 as a slight color change from an earlier flag adopted from 1949. The triangular edges represent Qatar as the 9th 'reconciled emirate'. The flag is similar to the flag of Bahrain, which has fewer points, a 3:5 proportion, and a red colour instead of maroon. Qatar's flag is the only national flag having a width more than twice its height.",
  ROU: "Capital: Bucharest. Currency: Romanian leu (RON). Languages listed as official or national: Romanian. People are called: Romanian. Area: 238,391 km2 (rank 81 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 46 N, 25 E. Population: about 19.1 million (2022). Highest point: Moldoveanu Peak (2544 m). National anthem: Deșteaptă-te, române!. Official languages and their families: Romanian (language families: Eastern Romance, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: semi-presidential system, unitary state. Lowest point: Black Sea. Internet domain: .ro. Calling code: +40. Flag: The national flag of Romania is a tricolor featuring three equal vertical bands colored blue, yellow, and red, with a width-to-length ratio of 2:3.",
  RUS: "Capital: Moscow. Currency: Russian ruble (RUB). Languages listed as official or national: Russian. People are called: Russian. Area: 17,098,242 km2 (rank 1 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 60 N, 100 E. Population: about 146.1 million (2025). Highest point: Mount Elbrus (5642 m). National anthem: State Anthem of the Russian Federation. Official languages and their families: Russian (language families: East Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: super-presidential republic. Lowest point: Caspian Sea. Internet domain: .ru, .su. Calling code: +7. Flag: The national flag of Russia is a tricolour of three equal horizontal bands: white on the top, blue in the middle, and red on the bottom.",
  RWA: "Capital: Kigali. Currency: Rwandan franc (RWF). Languages listed as official or national: English, French, Kinyarwanda. People are called: Rwandan. Area: 26,338 km2 (rank 144 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 2 S, 30 E. Population: about 14.6 million (2025). Highest point: Mount Karisimbi (4507 m). National anthem: Rwanda Nziza. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Kinyarwanda (language families: Great Lakes Bantu, Northeast Bantu, East Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); Swahili (language families: Bantu, Sabaki, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Northeast Coast Bantu, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Northeast Bantu, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, East Bantu). Form of government: semi-presidential system. Lowest point: Ruzizi River. Internet domain: .rw. Calling code: +250. Flag: The national flag of Rwanda is a horizontal tricolour consisting of a light blue top half, two equal bands of yellow and green in the bottom half, and a golden sun in the upper fly-side corner. It was adopted on 31 December 2001. Its design is defined in the Rwandan constitution and regulations regarding its use and manufacture are outlined in the country's national flag law.",
  SAU: "Capital: Riyadh. Currency: Saudi riyal (SAR). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Saudi Arabian. Area: 2,149,690 km2 (rank 12 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 25 N, 45 E. Highest point: Jabal Sawda (3207 m). National anthem: Chant of the Saudi Nation. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: monarchy, absolute monarchy, islamic theocracy. Lowest point: Red Sea. Internet domain: .sa. Calling code: +966. Flag: The national flag of Saudi Arabia is a green background with Arabic inscription and a sword in white, with an inscription of the shahada. The current design has been used by the government of Saudi Arabia since 15 March 1973.",
  SDN: "Capital: Khartoum. Currency: Sudanese pound (SDG). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, English. People are called: Sudanese. Area: 1,886,068 km2 (rank 15 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 N, 30 E. Population: about 48.9 million (2025). Highest point: Deriba Caldera (3042 m). National anthem: Nahnu Jund Allah Jund Al-watan. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Red Sea. Internet domain: .sd. Calling code: +249. Flag: The flag of Sudan used since 20 May 1970 consists of a horizontal red-white-black tricolour with a green triangle at the hoist. The flag is based on the Arab Liberation Flag of the Egyptian Revolution of 1952. Upon gaining independence in 1956, Sudan, adopted a horizontal tricolour of blue, yellow and green. This flag was abandoned after the 1969 military coup of Gaafar Nimeiry.",
  SEN: "Capital: Dakar. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Senegalese. Area: 196,722 km2 (rank 86 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 14 N, 14 W. Population: about 18.1 million (2026). Highest point: Népin Cliff (531 m). National anthem: Le Lion rouge. Official languages and their families: Badyara (language families: Atlantic–Congo, Tenda, Atlantic, Senegambian, Niger–Congo); Balanta (language families: Bak, Atlantic, Northern Atlantic, Atlantic–Congo, Niger–Congo); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic); Wolof (language families: Senegambian, Atlantic, Atlantic–Congo, Niger–Congo). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .sn. Calling code: +221. Flag: The national flag of Senegal is a tricolour consisting of three vertical green, yellow and red bands charged with a five-pointed green star at the centre. Adopted in 1960 to replace the flag of the Mali Federation, it has been the flag of the Republic of Senegal since the country gained independence that year. The present and previous flags were inspired by the French tricolour, which flew over Senegal until 1960.",
  SGP: "Capital: Singapore. Currency: Singapore dollar (SGD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Malay, Tamil, Chinese. People are called: Singaporean. Area: 710 km2 (rank 176 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1.36666666 N, 103.8 E. Highest point: Bukit Timah (164 m). National anthem: Onward Singapore. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Standard Chinese (language families: Sino-Tibetan, Dené–Caucasian, Sino-Austronesian, Sino-Tibetan-Indo-European); Tamil (language families: Tamil languages, Tamil–Malayalam, Tamil–Kannada, South Dravidian, Dravidian); Malay (language families: Malayan, Malayic, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Singapore Strait. Internet domain: .sg. Calling code: +65. Flag: The flag of Singapore was adopted in 1959, the year Singapore became self-governing within the British Empire. Designed by a government committee led by deputy prime minister Toh Chin Chye, it remained as the national flag upon the country's independence from Malaysia on 9 August 1965. The design is a horizontal bicolour of red above white, overlaid in the canton by a white crescent moon facing a pentagon of five small white five-pointed stars. The elements of the flag denote a young nation on the ascendant, universal brotherhood and equality, and national ideals.",
  SLB: "Capital: Honiara. Currency: Solomon Islands dollar (SBD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Solomon Islander. Area: 28,896 km2 (rank 139 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 S, 159 E. Highest point: Mount Popomanaseu (2335 m). National anthem: God Save Our Solomon Islands. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .sb. Calling code: +677. Flag: The flag of Solomon Islands consists of a thin yellow diagonal stripe from the lower hoist-side corner, with a blue upper triangle and green lower triangle, and the canton charged with five white stars. Adopted in 1977 to replace the British Blue Ensign defaced with the arms of the protectorate, it has been the flag of Solomon Islands since 18 November of that year, eight months before the country gained independence. Although the number of provinces has since increased, the number of stars on the flag that originally represented them remained unchanged.",
  SLE: "Capital: Freetown. Currency: Sierra Leonean leone (SLL). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Sierra Leonean. Area: 71,740 km2 (rank 117 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8.5 N, 11.5 W. Highest point: Mount Bintumani (1945 m). National anthem: High We Exalt Thee, Realm of the Free. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Krio. Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .sl. Calling code: +232. Flag: The national flag of Sierra Leone is a tricolour consisting of three horizontal green, white and blue bands. It was adopted in 1961, Sierra Leone's independence year, to replace the British Blue Ensign defaced with the arms of the Crown Colony of Sierra Leone.",
  SLV: "Capital: San Salvador. Currency: United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Salvadoran. Area: 21,041 km2 (rank 148 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.83333333 N, 88.91666666 W. Population: about 6.0 million (2024). Highest point: Cerro El Pital (2730 m). National anthem: National anthem of El Salvador. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic, presidential system, representative democracy. Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .sv. Calling code: +503. Flag: The national flag of El Salvador, officially named the Bandera Magna, is a horizontal triband of blue-white-blue, with the national coat of arms centered and entirely contained within the central white stripe. The current flag was adopted by the Legislative Assembly of El Salvador on 24 March 1916, and its design is established by the Law of National Symbols approved in 1972. The flag is inspired by the flag of the Federal Republic of Central America, which itself was inspired by the flag of Argentina. From 1865 to 1912, the flag of El Salvador resembled that of the United States, consisting of a red canton, nine alternating blue and white stripes, and white stars in the canton equal to the number of departments.",
  SMR: "Capital: City of San Marino. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Italian. People are called: Sammarinese. Area: 61 km2 (rank 191 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 43.76666666 N, 12.41666666 E. Population: about 0.0 million (2026). Highest point: Monte Titano (739 m). Official languages and their families: Italian (language families: Italo-Romance, Italo-Dalmatian, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: diarchy, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Ausa River. Internet domain: .sm. Calling code: +378. Flag: The national flag of San Marino is formed by two equal horizontal bands of white (top) and light blue with the national coat of arms superimposed in the center; the coat of arms has a shield with a closed crown on top, flanked by an oak and laurel wreath, with a scroll below bearing the word LIBERTAS (Freedom). The two colours of the flag represent peace (white) and liberty.",
  SOM: "Capital: Mogadishu. Currency: Somali shilling (SOS). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, Somali. People are called: Somali. Area: 637,657 km2 (rank 41 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 10 N, 49 E. Population: about 18.1 million (2026). Highest point: Shimbiris (2460 m). National anthem: Qolobaa Calankeed. Official languages and their families: Somali (language families: Somali languages, Macro-Somali, Omo–Tana, Lowland East Cushitic, East Cushitic, Cushitic, Afroasiatic); Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .so. Calling code: +252. Flag: The national flag of Somalia was adopted on October 12, 1954, and was designed by Mohammed Awale Liban. The flag was initially used within the Trust Territory of Somaliland before being adopted by the short-lived State of Somaliland and the Somali Republic. It is an ethnic flag for the Somali people. The white five-pointed star represents the five regions in which the Somalis reside.",
  SRB: "Capital: Belgrade. Currency: Serbian dinar (RSD). Languages listed as official or national: Serbian. People are called: Serbian. Area: 88,361 km2 (rank 111 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 44 N, 21 E. Population: about 6.6 million (2024). Highest point: Velika Rudoka (2658 m). National anthem: Bože pravde. Official languages and their families: Serbian (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Timok. Internet domain: .rs. Calling code: +381. Flag: The flag of Serbia, also known as the Tricolour, is a tricolour consisting of three equal horizontal bands, red on the top, blue in the middle, and white on the bottom, with the lesser coat of arms towards the hoist of centre.",
  SSD: "Capital: Juba. Currency: South Sudanese pound (SSP). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: South Sudanese. Area: 619,745 km2 (rank 43 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 7 N, 30 E. Highest point: Kinyeti (3187 m). National anthem: South Sudan Oyee!. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: federal republic. Lowest point: White Nile. Internet domain: .ss. Calling code: +211. Flag: The flag of South Sudan was adopted following the signing of the Comprehensive Peace Agreement that ended the Second Sudanese Civil War. A different version of the flag was previously used as the flag of the Sudan People's Liberation Movement. The flag of South Sudan predates the country, as the flag was adopted in 2005, while the country became independent in 2011.",
  STP: "Capital: São Tomé. Currency: São Tomé and Príncipe dobra (STN). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese. People are called: Sao Tomean. Area: 964 km2 (rank 171 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1 N, 7 E. Highest point: Pico de São Tomé (2024 m). National anthem: Independência total. Official languages and their families: Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .st. Calling code: +239. Flag: The national flag of São Tomé and Príncipe is a horizontal triband of green, yellow, and green, with a red isosceles triangle at the hoist and two five-pointed black stars on the yellow band. The flag's aspect ratio is 1:2 and the ratio of the bands are 2:3:2. The flag was adopted upon São Tomé and Príncipe's independence from Portugal on 12 July 1975. The design is based on, and nearly identical to, the first flag of the Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe (MLSTP), which led the country to its independence.",
  SUR: "Capital: Paramaribo. Currency: Surinamese dollar (SRD). Languages listed as official or national: Dutch. People are called: Surinamer. Area: 163,820 km2 (rank 90 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 4 N, 56 W. Highest point: Julianatop (1280 m). National anthem: God zij met ons Suriname. Official languages and their families: Dutch (language families: Low Franconian, Franconian, West Germanic languages, High German, West Central German, Weser-Rhine Germanic, South Germanic, Old Germanic, North Sea Germanic, Central German, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic). Form of government: republic. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .sr. Calling code: +597. Flag: The national flag of Suriname was legally adopted on 25 November 1975, upon the independence of Suriname from the Netherlands.",
  SVK: "Capital: Bratislava. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Slovak. People are called: Slovak. Area: 49,037 km2 (rank 127 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 48.66666666 N, 19.5 E. Population: about 5.4 million (2025). Highest point: Gerlachovský štít (2654 m). National anthem: Nad Tatrou sa blýska. Official languages and their families: Slovak (language families: West Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Bodrog. Internet domain: .sk. Calling code: +421. Flag: The current form of the national flag of the Slovak Republic was adopted by Slovakia's Constitution, which came into force on 3 September 1992. The flag of Slovakia, like many other flags of Slavic nations, uses the Pan-Slavic colours. In the hoist side of the flag is Slovakia's national coat of arms.",
  SVN: "Capital: Ljubljana. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Slovene. People are called: Slovene. Area: 20,273 km2 (rank 150 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 46.11666666 N, 14.81666666 E. Population: about 2.1 million (2022). Highest point: Triglav (2864 m). National anthem: Zdravljica. Official languages and their families: Slovene (language families: Western South Slavic, South Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: democratic republic. Lowest point: Adriatic Sea. Internet domain: .si. Calling code: +386. Flag: The national flag of Slovenia features three equal horizontal bands of white (top), blue, and red, with the coat of arms of Slovenia located in the upper hoist side of the flag centred in the white and blue bands. The coat of arms is a shield with the image of Mount Triglav, Slovenia's highest peak, in white against a blue background at the centre; beneath it are two wavy blue lines representing the Adriatic Sea and local rivers, and above it are three six-pointed golden stars arranged in an inverted triangle which are taken from the coat of arms of the Counts of Celje, the great Slovene dynastic house of the late 14th and early 15th centuries.",
  SWE: "Capital: Stockholm. Currency: Swedish krona (SEK). Languages listed as official or national: Swedish. People are called: Swedish. Area: 450,295 km2 (rank 55 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 62 N, 15 E. Population: about 10.6 million (2025). Highest point: Kebnekaise (2097 m). National anthem: Du gamla, du fria. Official languages and their families: Swedish (language families: East Scandinavian, North Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, representative democracy. Lowest point: Kristianstad. Internet domain: .se. Calling code: +46. Flag: The national flag of Sweden consists of a yellow or gold Nordic cross on a field of light blue. The Nordic cross design traditionally represents Christianity. The design and colours of the Swedish flag are believed to have been inspired by the present coat of arms of Sweden of 1442, which is blue divided quarterly by a cross pattée of gold. Blue and yellow have been used as Swedish colours at least since Magnus III's royal coat of arms of 1275.",
  SWZ: "Capital: Lobamba. Currency: Swazi lilangeni (SZL), South African rand (ZAR). Languages listed as official or national: English, Swazi. People are called: Swazi. Area: 17,364 km2 (rank 153 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 26.5 S, 31.5 E. Population: about 1.2 million (2023). Highest point: Emlembe (1862 m). National anthem: Nkulunkulu Mnikati wetibusiso temaSwati. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Swazi (language families: Nguni, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo). Form of government: absolute monarchy. Lowest point: Maputo River. Internet domain: .sz. Calling code: +268. Flag: The flag of Eswatini was adopted on 6 October 1968. After Eswatini gained its independence from the United Kingdom one month before. The design by King Sobhuza II features a black and white shield called the Nguni shield, with a staff and two spears, on a field of blue, yellow, and red horizontal bands.",
  SYC: "Capital: Victoria. Currency: Seychellois rupee (SCR). Languages listed as official or national: Seychellois Creole, English, French. People are called: Seychellois. Area: 452 km2 (rank 181 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 4.58333333 S, 55.66666666 E. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: Morne Seychellois (905 m). National anthem: Koste Seselwa. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Seychellois Creole (language families: French-based creole languages); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .sc. Calling code: +248. Flag: The national flag of Seychelles was adopted on 8 January 1996. The current flag is the third used by the country since its independence from the United Kingdom on 29 June 1976. The colours used in the current flag are the official colours of two of the country's major political parties: the Seychelles People's United Party and the Seychelles Democratic Party.",
  SYR: "Capital: Damascus. Currency: Syrian pound (SYP). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Syrian. Area: 185,180 km2 (rank 87 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 35 N, 38 E. Population: about 23.0 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Hermon (2814 m). National anthem: Humat ad-Diyar. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: provisional government. Lowest point: Sea of Galilee. Internet domain: .sy. Calling code: +963. Flag: The national flag of Syria, commonly known as the Syrian flag or the flag of the Syrian Arab Republic, is a rectangular tricolour featuring three stars. It consists of three equal horizontal stripes of green, white, and black, from top to bottom, with three red five-pointed stars centered in the white stripe, which has a length equal to 2.3 its width.",
  TCD: "Capital: N'Djamena. Currency: Central African CFA franc (XAF). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic, French. People are called: Chadian. Area: 1,284,000 km2 (rank 20 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 N, 19 E. Population: about 19.3 million (2023). Highest point: Emi Koussi (3445 m). National anthem: La Tchadienne. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic); French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Bodélé Depression. Internet domain: .td. Calling code: +235. Flag: The national flag of Chad is a vertical tricolour of indigo, yellow, and red. It was adopted on 6 November 1959, almost a year after the founding of the autonomous Republic of Chad. Since the 1990s, its similarity to the flag of Romania has caused international discussion.",
  TGO: "Capital: Lomé. Currency: West African CFA franc (XOF). Languages listed as official or national: French. People are called: Togolese. Area: 56,785 km2 (rank 123 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 N, 1.16666666 E. Population: about 12.4 million (2026). Highest point: Mount Agou (986 m). National anthem: Terre de nos aïeux. Official languages and their families: French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Bight of Benin. Internet domain: .tg. Calling code: +228. Flag: The national flag of Togo consists of five horizontal stripes, alternating between green and yellow, with a red square bearing a five-pointed white star in the canton. It is one of many African flags that use the pan-African colours of green, yellow, and red. Togolese artist Paul Ahyi designed the flag in 1960, just before its adoption on 27 April of that year, coinciding with Togo's proclamation of independence. When Togo was an autonomous republic within the French Union, it flew a green flag with the French tricolour in the canton and two five-pointed yellow stars, one in the lower hoist and one in the upper fly.",
  THA: "Capital: Bangkok. Currency: Thai baht (THB). Languages listed as official or national: Thai. People are called: Thai. Area: 513,120 km2 (rank 50 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 N, 100 E. Highest point: Doi Inthanon (2565 m). National anthem: Thai National Anthem. Official languages and their families: Thai (language families: Southwestern Tai, Tai, Kam–Tai, Kra–Dai, Austro-Tai). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, parliamentary monarchy. Lowest point: Andaman Sea. Internet domain: .th. Calling code: +66. Flag: The flag of Thailand consists of five horizontal stripes of red, white, blue, white and red, with the central blue stripe being twice as wide as each of the other four. The design was adopted on 28 September 1917, according to the royal decree issued by King Vajiravudh. Since 2016, that day is a national day of importance in Thailand celebrating the flag.",
  TJK: "Capital: Dushanbe. Currency: Tajikistani somoni (TJS). Languages listed as official or national: Russian, Tajik. People are called: Tadzhik. Area: 143,100 km2 (rank 94 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 39 N, 71 E. Highest point: Ismoil Somoni Peak (7495 m). National anthem: National anthem of Tajikistan. Official languages and their families: Tajik (language families: Persian, Southwestern Iranian, Western Iranian, Iranian, Indo-Iranian, Indo-European). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Syr Darya. Internet domain: .tj. Calling code: +992. Flag: The national flag of Tajikistan was adopted in November 1992, replacing the flag of the Tajik Soviet Socialist Republic of 1953. The flag of Tajikistan is an unequal horizontal tricolour of red, white, and green, defaced with a yellow crown surmounted by an arc of seven stars at the centre. It has a width ratio of 2:3:2. The tricolour preserves the choice of colours from the former Tajik Soviet flag, as well as the 1:2 proportions.",
  TKM: "Capital: Ashgabat. Currency: Turkmenistan manat (TMT). Languages listed as official or national: Russian, Turkmen. People are called: Turkmen. Area: 488,100 km2 (rank 52 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 40 N, 60 E. Highest point: Aýrybaba (3139 m). National anthem: National anthem of Turkmenistan. Official languages and their families: Turkmen (language families: Eastern Oghuz, Oghuz, Common Turkic languages, Turkic). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Turan Depression. Internet domain: .tm. Calling code: +993. Flag: The national flag of Turkmenistan features a white crescent and five stars representing the five regions of the country and the Five Pillars of Islam. Placed upon a green field is a symbolic representation of the country's famous carpet industry. It was introduced as the flag of Turkmenistan on 27 September 1992 to replace the Soviet-era flag. The modified version with a 2:3 ratio was adopted on 24 January 2001. State Flag and Constitution Day is celebrated on 18 May.",
  TLS: "Capital: Dili. Currency: United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: Portuguese, Tetum. People are called: East Timorese. Area: 14,874 km2 (rank 154 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8.83333333 S, 125.91666666 E. Population: about 1.4 million (2024). Highest point: Tatamailau (2963 m). National anthem: Pátria. Official languages and their families: Tetum (language families: Timoric, Central Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian); Portuguese (language families: Galician–Portuguese, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: republic, semi-presidential system, unitary state. Lowest point: Timor Sea. Internet domain: .tl. Calling code: +670. Flag: The national flag of Timor-Leste consists of a red field with the black isosceles triangle based on the hoist-side bearing a white five-pointed star in the center superimposed on the larger yellow triangle, also based on the hoist-side, that extends to the center of the flag.",
  TON: "Capital: Nuku'alofa. Currency: Tongan paʻanga (TOP). Languages listed as official or national: English, Tongan. People are called: Tongan. Area: 747 km2 (rank 175 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 20 S, 175 W. Highest point: Kao (1030 m). National anthem: Ko e fasi 'o e tu'i 'o e 'Otu Tonga. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Tongan (language families: Tongic, Polynesian, Central Pacific, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .to. Calling code: +676. Flag: The national flag of Tonga consists of a red field with a white canton charged with a red couped cross. Adopted in 1875 after being officially enshrined into the nation's constitution, it has been the flag of the Kingdom of Tonga since that year. The constitution stipulates that the national flag can never be changed.",
  TTO: "Capital: Port of Spain. Currency: Trinidad and Tobago dollar (TTD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Trinidadian. Area: 5,130 km2 (rank 165 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 11 N, 61 W. Highest point: El Cerro del Aripo (940 m). National anthem: Forged from the Love of Liberty. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .tt. Calling code: +1868. Flag: The flag of Trinidad and Tobago was adopted upon independence from the United Kingdom on 31 August 1962. Designed by Carlisle Chang (1921–2001), the flag of Trinidad and Tobago was chosen by the independence committee of 1962. Red, black and white symbolise fire, earth and water.",
  TUN: "Capital: Tunis. Currency: Tunisian dinar (TND). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Tunisian. Area: 163,610 km2 (rank 91 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 34 N, 9 E. Population: about 12.4 million (2026). Highest point: Jebel ech Chambi (1544 m). National anthem: Humat Al Hima. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: semi-presidential system, presidential system, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Shatt al Gharsah. Internet domain: .tn. Calling code: +216. Flag: The national flag of Tunisia is a rectangular panel of red color with an aspect ratio of 2:3. In the center of the cloth in a white disk is placed a red crescent, surrounding a red five-pointed star on three sides. The Tunisian Bey Hussein II decided to create a flag for Tunisia, close in appearance to the modern one, after the Battle of Navarino on 20 October 1827; in 1831 he was officially approved. In that form, the flag existed during the French protectorate, and on 1 June 1959, it was proclaimed the state flag of the Republic of Tunisia. On 30 June 1999, the proportions and design of the flag were clarified by a special law. The general appearance of the flag remained virtually unchanged.",
  TUR: "Capital: Ankara. Currency: Turkish lira (TRY). Languages listed as official or national: Turkish. People are called: Turkish. Area: 783,562 km2 (rank 36 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 39 N, 35 E. Population: about 85.4 million (2023). Highest point: Mount Ararat (5137 m). National anthem: İstiklâl Marşı. Official languages and their families: Turkish (language families: Western Oghuz, Oghuz, Common Turkic languages, Turkic). Form of government: presidential system, unitary state. Lowest point: Black Sea. Internet domain: .tr. Calling code: +90. Flag: The national flag of Turkey, officially the Turkish flag, features a white crescent and star in a red background on its emblem, based on the 18th-century flag of the Ottoman Empire. The flag is often called 'the red flag', and is referred to as 'the red banner' in the Turkish national anthem. The measures, geometric proportions, and exact tone of red of the flag of Turkey were legally standardized with the Turkish Flag Law on 29 May 1936.",
  TUV: "Capital: Funafuti. Currency: Australian dollar (AUD), Tuvaluan dollar (TVD). Languages listed as official or national: English, Tuvaluan. People are called: Tuvaluan. Area: 26 km2 (rank 192 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 S, 178 E. Population: about 0.0 million (2022). Highest point: Niulakita (5 m). National anthem: Tuvalu mo te Atua. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Tuvaluan (language families: Ellicean, Nuclear Polynesian, Polynesian, Central Pacific, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .tv. Calling code: +688. Flag: The national flag of Tuvalu is a light blue field with the Union Jack in the canton and nine yellow five-pointed stars on the fly (right) half of the flag. The nine stars represent the nine islands of Tuvalu, while the Union Jack symbolises the country's connections to the United Kingdom and the Commonwealth. The flag was originally adopted on 1 October 1978, the day Tuvalu became independent.",
  TZA: "Capital: Dodoma. Currency: Tanzanian shilling (TZS). Languages listed as official or national: English, Swahili. People are called: Tanzanian. Area: 945,087 km2 (rank 30 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 6 S, 35 E. Population: about 61.7 million (2022). Highest point: Mount Kibo (5895 m). National anthem: Mungu ibariki Afrika. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Swahili (language families: Bantu, Sabaki, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Northeast Coast Bantu, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Northeast Bantu, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, East Bantu). Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .tz. Calling code: +255. Flag: The national flag of Tanzania consists of a gold-edged black bend, divided diagonally from the lower hoist-side corner, with a green upper triangle and light blue lower triangle. Adopted in 1964 to replace the individual flags of Tanganyika and Zanzibar, it has been the flag of the United Republic of Tanzania since the two states merged that year. The design of the present flag incorporates the elements from the two former flags. It is one of a relatively small number of national flags incorporating a diagonal line, with other examples including the DR Congo, Republic of the Congo, Namibia, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Trinidad and Tobago and Brunei.",
  UGA: "Capital: Kampala. Currency: Ugandan shilling (UGX). Languages listed as official or national: English, Swahili. People are called: Ugandan. Area: 241,550 km2 (rank 79 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 1 N, 32 E. Highest point: Mount Stanley (5109 m). National anthem: Oh Uganda, Land of Beauty. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Swahili (language families: Bantu, Sabaki, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Northeast Coast Bantu, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Northeast Bantu, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, East Bantu). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Lake Albert. Internet domain: .ug. Calling code: +256. Flag: The national flag of Uganda was adopted on 9 October 1962, the day the nation became independent from the United Kingdom. It consists of six equal horizontal bands, from top to bottom, of black, yellow, red, black, yellow, and red, with a white disc containing a crested crane superimposed (charged) at the centre. The National Flag and Armorial Ensigns Act specifies prohibited uses of the flag.",
  UKR: "Capital: Kyiv. Currency: Ukrainian hryvnia (UAH). Languages listed as official or national: Ukrainian. People are called: Ukrainian. Area: 603,500 km2 (rank 44 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 49 N, 32 E. Population: about 41.2 million (2022). Highest point: Hoverla (2061 m). National anthem: Shche ne vmerla Ukrainy i slava, i volia. Official languages and their families: Ukrainian (language families: East Slavic, Slavic, Indo-European, Balto-Slavic). Form of government: semi-presidential system. Lowest point: Kuialnyk Liman. Internet domain: .ua. Calling code: +380. Flag: The national flag of Ukraine consists of equally sized horizontal bands of blue and yellow.",
  URY: "Capital: Montevideo. Currency: Uruguayan peso (UYU). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Uruguayan. Area: 181,034 km2 (rank 89 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 33 S, 56 W. Population: about 3.4 million (2023). Highest point: Cerro Catedral (514 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Uruguay. Form of government: republic, presidential system, participatory democracy. Lowest point: Atlantic Ocean. Internet domain: .uy. Calling code: +598. Flag: The national flag of Uruguay, officially known as the National Pavilion, is the most important of the three official flags of Uruguay along with the Artigas flag and the flag of the Treinta y Tres. It has a field of nine equal horizontal stripes alternating white and blue. The canton is white, charged with the Sun of May, from which 16 rays extend, alternating between triangular and wavy. The flag was first adopted by law on 18 December 1828, and had 19 alternating stripes of white and blue until 11 July 1830, when a new law reduced the number of alternating stripes to nine. The flag was designed by Joaquín Suárez.",
  USA: "Capital: Washington D.C.. Currency: United States dollar (USD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: American. Area: 9,372,610 km2 (rank 4 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 38 N, 97 W. Population: about 340.1 million (2024). Highest point: Denali (6190 m). National anthem: The Star-Spangled Banner. Official languages and their families: Hawaiian (language families: Marquesic, Nuclear Polynesian, Polynesian, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Central Pacific, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Oceanic, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); Chamorro (language families: Western Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Carolinian (language families: Chuukic, Chuukic–Pohnpeic, Micronesian, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian); Samoan (language families: Ellicean, Nuclear Polynesian, Polynesian, Central Pacific, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Form of government: constitutional republic, democratic republic. Lowest point: Badwater Basin. Internet domain: .us. Calling code: +1. Flag: The national flag of the United States of America, often referred to as the American flag or the U.S. flag, consists of thirteen horizontal stripes, alternating red and white starting with red, with a blue rectangle in the canton bearing fifty small, white, five-pointed stars arranged in nine offset horizontal rows, where rows of six stars alternate with rows of five stars. The 50 stars on the flag form a constellation, representing the 50 U.S. states united, while the 13 stripes represent the thirteen British colonies that won independence in the American Revolutionary War.",
  UZB: "Capital: Tashkent. Currency: Uzbekistani soʻm (UZS). Languages listed as official or national: Russian, Uzbek. People are called: Uzbekistani. Area: 447,400 km2 (rank 56 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 41 N, 64 E. Highest point: Khazret Sultan (4643 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Uzbekistan. Official languages and their families: Uzbek (language families: Western Karluk, Karluk, Common Turkic languages, Turkic). Form of government: presidential system. Lowest point: Sarygamysh Lake. Internet domain: .uz. Calling code: +998. Flag: The national flag of Uzbekistan, officially the state flag of the Republic of Uzbekistan, consists of a horizontal triband of dark azure blue, white and dark green, separated by two thin red fimbriations, with a white crescent moon and twelve white stars in the canton. Adopted in 1991 to replace the flag of the Uzbek Soviet Socialist Republic, it has been the flag of the Republic of Uzbekistan since the country gained independence in that same year. The design of the present flag was based on the former one.",
  VAT: "Capital: Vatican City. Currency: Euro (EUR). Languages listed as official or national: Italian, Latin. People are called: Vatican. Area: 0 km2 (rank 195 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 41.9 N, 12.45 E. Population: about 0.0 million (2024). Highest point: Vatican Hill (75 m). National anthem: Inno e Marcia Pontificale. Official languages and their families: Latin (language families: Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Italian (language families: Italo-Romance, Italo-Dalmatian, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic). Form of government: constitutional monarchy, theocracy, papacy. Lowest point: Saint Peter's Square. Internet domain: .va. Calling code: +3. Flag: The flag of Vatican City, also referred to as the flag of the Holy See, consists of vertical bicolour of yellow and white, with the white half charged with the emblem of the Holy See. It was adopted in 1929, the year Pope Pius XI signed the Lateran Treaty with Italy, creating the new independent state of Vatican City.",
  VCT: "Capital: Kingstown. Currency: Eastern Caribbean dollar (XCD). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Saint Vincentian. Area: 389 km2 (rank 184 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.25 N, 61.2 W. Population: about 0.1 million (2023). Highest point: La Soufrière (1220 m). National anthem: Saint Vincent, Land so Beautiful. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: constitutional monarchy. Lowest point: Caribbean Sea. Internet domain: .vc. Calling code: +1784. Flag: The flag of Saint Vincent and the Grenadines is a Canadian pale triband consisting of blue, gold, and green bands charged with three green diamonds at the middle. Adopted in 1985 to replace a similar design used from the time of independence, it has been the flag of Saint Vincent since that year. The design of the present flag entailed substituting the country's coat of arms on a breadfruit leaf with the diamonds. They are a reference to both the letter 'V', which is the first letter of the country's name, and its nickname as the 'Gems of the Antilles' and 'Jewels of the Caribbean'. Accordingly, the flag itself has been given the moniker of 'The Gems'.",
  VEN: "Capital: Caracas. Currency: Venezuelan bolívar soberano (VES). Languages listed as official or national: Spanish. People are called: Venezuelan. Area: 916,445 km2 (rank 32 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 8 N, 66 W. Population: about 31.3 million (2024). Highest point: Pico Bolívar (4978 m). National anthem: gloria al bravo pueblo. Official languages and their families: Spanish (language families: Castilian, West Iberian, Iberian Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Italic, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic); Venezuelan Sign Language. Form of government: presidential system, federal republic. Lowest point: Lagunillas. Internet domain: .ve. Calling code: +58. Flag: The current national flag of Venezuela was introduced in 2006. The basic design includes a horizontal tricolour of yellow, blue, and red, dating to the original flag introduced in 1811, in the Venezuelan War of Independence.",
  VNM: "Capital: Hanoi. Currency: Vietnamese đồng (VND). Languages listed as official or national: Vietnamese. People are called: Vietnamese. Area: 331,212 km2 (rank 66 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 16.16666666 N, 107.83333333 E. Highest point: Fansipan (3143 m). National anthem: Tiến Quân Ca. Official languages and their families: Vietnamese (language families: Vietic, Mon-Khmer languages, Austroasiatic). Form of government: republic, one-party state, unitary state. Lowest point: South China Sea. Internet domain: .vn. Calling code: +84. Flag: The national flag of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, previously presenting the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, was originally designed in 1940 and first used during a failed worker and peasant uprising against the French colonialists in Cochinchina that year. The red background symbolizes revolution and bloodshed. The golden star symbolizes the soul of the nation and the five points of the star represents the five main classes in Vietnamese society—intellectuals, farmers, workers, entrepreneurs, and soldiers.",
  VUT: "Capital: Port Vila. Currency: Vanuatu vatu (VUV). Languages listed as official or national: Bislama, English, French. People are called: Ni-Vanuatu. Area: 12,189 km2 (rank 157 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 16 S, 167 E. Highest point: Mount Tabwemasana (1879 m). National anthem: Yumi, Yumi. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Bislama; French (language families: Oïl, Gallo-Rhaetian, Gallo-Romance, Gallo-Iberian, Western Romance, Italo-Western, Romance, Latino-Faliscan, Indo-European, Italo-Celtic, Italic). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .vu. Calling code: +678. Flag: The national flag of Vanuatu was adopted on 18 February 1980.",
  WSM: "Capital: Apia. Currency: Samoan tālā (WST). Languages listed as official or national: English, Samoan. People are called: Samoan. Area: 2,842 km2 (rank 167 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 13.58333333 S, 172.33333333 W. Highest point: Silisili (1858 m). National anthem: The Banner of Freedom. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Samoan (language families: Ellicean, Nuclear Polynesian, Polynesian, Central Pacific, Central–Eastern Oceanic, Oceanic, Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Austronesian, Austric, Austro-Tai, Sino-Austronesian). Lowest point: Pacific Ocean. Internet domain: .ws. Calling code: +685. Flag: The flag of Samoa is a red field with a blue canton bearing five white five-pointed stars arranged to form the Southern Cross constellation. Paramount chiefs Malietoa Tanumafili II and Tupua Tamasese Meaʻole designed the flag with four stars in 1948, when Samoa was under the stewardship of New Zealand as the Territory of Western Samoa. The smaller fifth star was added in 1949.",
  YEM: "Capital: Sana'a. Currency: Yemeni rial (YER). Languages listed as official or national: Arabic. People are called: Yemeni. Area: 527,968 km2 (rank 49 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 N, 48 E. Population: about 34.4 million (2026). Highest point: Jabal an Nabi Shu'ayb (3666 m). National anthem: National anthem of Yemen. Official languages and their families: Arabic (language families: Afroasiatic, Semitic). Form of government: republic, presidential system. Lowest point: Arabian Sea. Internet domain: .ye. Calling code: +967. Flag: The national flag of Yemen is the official flag of the Republic of Yemen. It was adopted on 22 May 1990, the day of the unification of Yemen. It resembles the Arab Liberation Flag that was used by the National Liberation Front. It served as the derivation for the flags of both North and South Yemen prior to their unification, and the distinctive elements of both former flags were removed in choosing the flag post-unification.",
  ZAF: "Capital: Pretoria and Bloemfontein and Cape Town. Currency: South African rand (ZAR). Languages listed as official or national: Afrikaans, English, Southern Ndebele, Northern Sotho, Southern Sotho, Swazi, Tswana, Tsonga, Venda, Xhosa, Zulu. People are called: South African. Area: 1,221,037 km2 (rank 24 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 29 S, 24 E. Population: about 62.0 million (2022). Highest point: Mafadi (3450 m). National anthem: national anthem of Mzansi. Official languages and their families: Southern Ndebele (language families: Bantu, Nguni, Zunda, Southern Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Northern Sotho (language families: Sotho–Tswana, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo); Tswana (language families: Sotho–Tswana, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Sesotho (language families: Sotho–Tswana, Bantu, Southern Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Swazi (language families: Nguni, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo); Tsonga (language families: Tswa-Ronga, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Xhosa (language families: Nguni, Zunda, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo); Venda (language families: Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo); Afrikaans (language families: Low Franconian, Franconian, West Germanic languages, High German, West Central German, Weser-Rhine Germanic, South Germanic, Old Germanic, North Sea Germanic, Central German, Indo-European, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic); South African Sign Language; Zulu (language families: Nguni, Zunda, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo). Form of government: apartheid, representative democracy, parliamentary republic. Lowest point: Indian Ocean. Internet domain: .za. Calling code: +27. Flag: The national flag of South Africa was designed in March 1994 and adopted on 27 April 1994, during South Africa's 1994 general election, to replace the previous flag used from 1928–1994.",
  ZMB: "Capital: Lusaka. Currency: Zambian kwacha (ZMW). Languages listed as official or national: English. People are called: Zambian. Area: 752,612 km2 (rank 38 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 15 S, 30 E. Population: about 19.6 million (2022). Highest point: Mafinga Central (2339 m). National anthem: Stand and Sing of Zambia, Proud and Free. Official languages and their families: English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European). Form of government: presidential system, representative democracy. Lowest point: Zambezi River. Internet domain: .zm. Calling code: +260. Flag: The national flag of Zambia was adopted upon independence on 24 October 1964, by the first Republican president, Dr. Kenneth David Kaunda. Before that, Zambia was the British protectorate of Northern Rhodesia and used a Blue Ensign as its flag.",
  ZWE: "Capital: Harare. Currency: Botswana pula (BWP), Chinese yuan (CNY), Euro (EUR), British pound (GBP), Indian rupee (INR), Japanese yen (JPY), United States dollar (USD), South African rand (ZAR), Zimbabwean bonds (ZWB). Languages listed as official or national: Chibarwe, English, Kalanga, Khoisan, Ndau, Northern Ndebele, Chewa, Shona, Sotho, Tonga, Tswana, Tsonga, Venda, Xhosa, Zimbabwean Sign Language. People are called: Zimbabwean. Area: 390,757 km2 (rank 60 of 195 by area). Rough center of the country: 20 S, 30 E. Population: about 15.2 million (2022). Highest point: Mount Nyangani (2592 m). National anthem: National Anthem of Zimbabwe. Official languages and their families: Chewa (language families: Nyasa, East Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo); Sesotho (language families: Sotho–Tswana, Bantu, Southern Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Northern Ndebele (language families: Bantu, Nguni, Zunda, Southern Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Xhosa (language families: Nguni, Zunda, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo); Khoisan; Tsonga (language families: Tswa-Ronga, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Tswana (language families: Sotho–Tswana, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); English (language families: Anglic, Anglo-Frisian, West Germanic languages, North Sea Germanic, Germanic languages, Northwest Germanic, Indo-European); Barwe (language families: Nyasa, East Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo); Kalanga (language families: Shona languages, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo); Venda (language families: Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo); Shona (language families: Shona languages, Southern Bantu, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Niger–Congo); Tonga (language families: Botatwe, East Bantu, Bantu, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo); Zimbabwe Sign Language; Ndau (language families: Southern Bantu, Bantu, Shona languages, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo); Nambya (language families: Southern Bantu, Shona languages, Bantu, Atlantic–Congo, Southern Bantoid, Niger–Congo, Bantoid, Benue–Congo, Volta–Congo). Lowest point: Runde River. Internet domain: .zw. Calling code: +263. Flag: The national flag of Zimbabwe consists of seven even horizontal stripes of green, gold, red and black with a white triangle containing a red five-pointed star with a Zimbabwe Bird. The present design was adopted on 18 April 1980.",
};
const STATE_FLAGS = {
  AL: "The flag of the U.S. state of Alabama was adopted by Act 383 of the Alabama Legislature on February 16, 1895, following the introduction of the bill by its designer, Representative John W. A. Sanford Jr. Its design features a crimson cross of St. Andrew on a field of white. A St. Andrew's cross, a diagonal cross known in vexillology as a saltire, represents the cross on which St. Andrew was crucified.",
  AK: "The flag of the U.S. state of Alaska displays eight gold stars, forming the Big Dipper and Polaris, on a dark blue field. The Big Dipper is an asterism in the constellation Ursa Major, which symbolizes a bear, indigenous to Alaska. As depicted on the flag, its stars can be used as a guide by the novice to locate Polaris and determine true north.",
  AZ: "The flag of Arizona is the official flag of the U.S. state of Arizona. It consists of 13 rays of red and gold on the top half. The center star signifies copper production; Arizona is rich in copper, producing more copper than other states in the country.",
  AR: "The state flag of the U.S. state of Arkansas, also known as the Arkansas flag, is a red banner charged with a large blue-bordered white lozenge (diamond). Twenty-nine five-pointed stars appear on the flag: twenty-five small white stars within the blue border, and four larger blue stars in the white diamond. The state's name appears in blue within the white lozenge, with one star above and three stars below. The star above and the two outer stars below point upwards; the inner star below points downwards. The flag was designed by Willie K. Hocker of Wabbaseka, a member of the United Daughters of the Confederacy and the Pine Bluff Chapter of the Arkansas Society of the Daughters of the American Revolution.",
  CA: "The flag of California, known as the Bear Flag, is the official flag of the U.S. state of California. The precursor of the flag was first flown during the 1846 Bear Flag Revolt and was also known as the Bear Flag, to which the flag draws most of its features. A predecessor, called the Lone Star Flag, was used in an 1836 independence movement; the red star element from that flag appears in the Bear Flag of today.",
  CO: "The flag of the U.S. state of Colorado was officially adopted on June 5, 1911. The flag, designed by Andrew Carlisle Carson, consists of a fess design of three horizontal stripes of equal width, with the top and bottom stripes colored blue, and the middle stripe colored white. A circular red 'C', filled with a golden disk, sits atop the stripes. All aspects of the flag contain symbolism related to the state, as the blue is meant to represent the sky, the gold the abundant sunshine the state receives, the white the snowcapped Rocky Mountains, and the red the 'ruddy' earth. The gold and white portions of the flag also represent the state's gold and silver mining industries, respectively.",
  CT: "The flag of the U.S. state of Connecticut is a white baroque shield with three grapevines, each bearing three bunches of purple grapes on a field of royal blue. The banner below the shield reads 'Qui Transtulit Sustinet,' Latin for 'He who transplanted sustains,' Connecticut's state motto. The flag dimensions are 5.5 feet (1.7 m) in length and 4.33 feet (1.32 m) in width.",
  DE: "The flag of Delaware consists of a buff-colored rhombus on a field of colonial blue, with the coat of arms of the state of Delaware inside the rhombus. Below the rhombus, the date December 7, 1787, declares the day on which Delaware became the first state to ratify the United States Constitution. The colors of the flag reflect the colors of the uniform of General George Washington.",
  FL: "The flag of Florida is the official flag of the U.S. state of Florida. The flag consists of a red saltire on a white background, with the state seal superimposed on the center. The current state flag was adopted on November 6, 1900, and has only been changed once on May 21, 1985 when the state seal was standardized.",
  GA: "The flag of the U.S. state of Georgia bears three horizontal stripes and features a blue canton containing a ring of 13 white stars that encircle the state's gold-colored coat of arms, with the motto of the United States, 'In God We Trust', below. Its current iteration was adopted on February 19, 2003.",
  HI: "The flag of Hawaii, also known as the Hawaiian flag, is the official flag of the U.S. state of Hawaii. Along with the flag of Texas, it is one of two state flags to have previously served as a national flag of an independent country. It consists of a field of eight horizontal stripes, in the sequence of white, red, blue, white, red, blue, white, red, with a British Union Jack depicted as a canton in the upper-left corner. The flag has been in use since 1845.",
  ID: "The seal of the U.S. State of Idaho was adopted in 1863 and redrawn several times before statehood in 1890. The first state Great Seal was designed in the 1890s by Emma Edwards Green, the only woman to design a U.S. state seal. That seal was used until 1957, when the seal was slightly redrawn by Paul B. Evans and the Caxton Printers, Ltd. at the request of the state government, in order to add more anthropocentric elements to the centered shield. The seal depicts a miner and a woman representing equality, liberty and justice. The symbols on the seal represent some of Idaho's natural resources: mines, forests, farmland and wildlife.",
  IL: "The great seal of the state of Illinois is the official emblem of the U.S. state of Illinois, and signifies the official nature of a document produced by the state. The present seal was designed and proposed in 1868 and officially adopted in 1869. It depicts in profile a bald eagle perched on a rock with wings uplifted and holding a shield, with a banner in its beak and sunrise over water in the background. It replaced an earlier seal that was almost the same as the Great Seal of the United States, adopted when Illinois became a state in 1818.",
  IN: "The flag of Indiana was adopted on May 31, 1917, as the third official flag of the U.S. state of Indiana. Designed by Paul Hadley, it has remained unchanged since its adoption, with specifications standardized in 1955. Prior to 1955, it was referred to as the state's official banner.",
  KS: "The flag of Kansas represents the U.S. state of Kansas. Designed by Hazel Avery in 1925 and adopted in 1927 after Kansas Adjutant General Milton R. McLean pushed the legislation that adopted the flag, its design features the Kansas state seal and a sunflower on a blue field. In 1961, the original design was modified to include the state’s name at the bottom of the flag.",
  KY: "The flag of the Commonwealth of Kentucky, a U.S. state, was adopted by the Kentucky General Assembly on March 26, 1918. It features a navy blue field with the Commonwealth's seal at its center, the words 'Commonwealth of Kentucky' above, and sprigs of goldenrod, the state flower, below. The seal depicts a pioneer and a statesman embracing, symbolizing the unity of frontiersmen and statesmen; while popular legend identifies them as Daniel Boone and Henry Clay, the official interpretation is that they represent all such figures collectively.",
  LA: "The flag of the U.S. state of Louisiana consists of a rectangular field of blue with the arms of Louisiana, a pelican vulning herself, in white in the center, with a ribbon beneath, also in white, containing in blue the state motto: 'Union Justice Confidence'. The flag was officially adopted July 1, 1912, and is often referred to as the Pelican flag.",
  ME: "The flag of Maine was adopted by the U.S. state on February 23, 1909. Its design consists of the state's coat of arms on a blue field. In the center of a heraldic shield, a moose rests under a tall pine tree. A farmer and seaman are meant to represent the traditional reliance on agriculture and the sea by the state. The North Star is intended to allude to the state motto: dirigo. Maine also uses an ensign with a different design; this flag was adopted on March 16, 1939.",
  MD: "The flag of Maryland is the official flag of the U.S. state of Maryland and the 17th-century heraldic banner of arms of Cecil, 2nd Baron Baltimore. It consists of the arms of his father George, 1st Baron Baltimore (1579–1632), quartered with those of his grandmother, heiress of the Crossland family. The flag was officially adopted by the Maryland General Assembly in 1904.",
  MA: "The flag of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts was adopted on March 18, 1908, and has only been changed once in 1971 with the removal of the reverse side. The flag features the coat of arms of the Commonwealth on a white field. The state currently has three official flags: a state flag, a governor's flag, and a 'naval and maritime flag'.",
  MI: "The flag of the U.S. state of Michigan is a coat of arms set on a dark blue field, as set forth by Michigan state law. As of 2025 Michigan has had three state flags, and the current flag was adopted on August 1, 1911. The governor has a variant of the flag with a white field instead of blue one. The state has an official flag month from June 14 through July 14.",
  MN: "The state flag of the U.S. state of Minnesota, adopted on May 11, 2024, features a dark blue field representing the night sky and the state's shape, an eight-pointed star symbolizing the North Star, and a light blue field representing the state's abundant waters; the shape of the star is based on the design featured on the rotunda floor the Minnesota State Capitol.",
  MS: "The flag of Mississippi is the official flag of the U.S. state of Mississippi. Its design consists of a white magnolia blossom surrounded by 21 stars and the words 'In God We Trust' written below, on a blue Canadian pale with two vertical gold borders on a red field. The topmost star, gold and composed of a pattern of five diamonds, 'represents ... the ... Native American tribes of ... Mississippi'; the other 20 stars are white and '[represent] Mississippi as the twentieth state' of the U.S. The current flag was adopted on January 11, 2021, replacing the previous design discontinued in 2020.",
  MO: "The flag of the U.S. state of Missouri was adopted on March 22, 1913, when governor Elliot Woolfolk Major signed the State flag act making it official. Its design consists of a triband of three equal horizontal stripes colored red, white, and blue, with the state coat of arms in the center.",
  NE: "The flag of the U.S. state of Nebraska, was adopted in 1925. It features a blue field with the Nebraskan state seal at its center, rendered in gold and silver colors. The flag was adopted following the introduction of a bill by Representative J. Lloyd McMaster in the former Nebraska House of Representatives.",
  NV: "The flag of the U.S. state of Nevada was adopted in 1929, when governor Fred B. Balzar signed into law a bill adopting the new flag. Its design consists of a solid cobalt blue field with, in the upper hoist quarter, two crossed sprays of sagebrush forming a half-wreath. Centered within the wreath is an upward-pointing silver star, with the state's name arched below it in evenly spaced golden-yellow letters. Above the wreath is a golden-yellow scroll touching its tips, bearing the words 'Battle Born', one of the state's mottos.",
  NH: "The seal of New Hampshire, codified by law in 1931, features the frigate USS Raleigh, encircled by a laurel wreath and nine stars. A granite boulder appears in the foreground, while water represents the harbor of Portsmouth. The U.S. state of New Hampshire has held two seals since it declared its independence from Great Britain on January 5, 1776.",
  NJ: "The coat of arms of New Jersey was adopted in 1777 and modified in 1928. It depicts a shield with three plows flanked by two women. Above the shield is a horse's head atop a knight's helmet. Below everything is a banner that reads Liberty and Prosperity 1776. The state seal of New Jersey depicts the coat of arms on a white disc surrounded by the state name. The flag of New Jersey was adopted on May 11, 1896. It features the coat of arms on a buff field.",
  NM: "The flag of the U.S. state of New Mexico, also referred to as the New Mexican flag and Zia Banner, is a state flag, consisting of a sacred red sun symbol of the Zia tribe on a field of gold (yellow). It was officially adopted on March 19, 1925 to highlight the state's Indigenous and Hispanic heritage: it combines a symbol of the Puebloan people, who have ancient roots in the state, with the colors of the flags of Spain, whose empire had established and ruled over “Nuevo México” for over two and a half centuries. The New Mexico flag is among the more distinctive and iconic in the U.S., and has been noted for its simple and aesthetically pleasing design. It is one of four U.S. state flags without the color blue and the only one among the four without the color white. New Mexico is one of only two U.S states that depicts indigenous iconography in its flag.",
  NY: "Flag of New York may refer to:Flag of the State of New York Flags of New York City",
  NC: "The flag of the state of North Carolina, often referred to as the North Carolina flag, N.C. flag, or North Star, is the state flag of the U.S. state of North Carolina.",
  ND: "The flag of North Dakota represents the U.S. state of North Dakota. Adopted on March 11, 1911, its design is an almost exact replica of the regimental banner carried by the state's troop contingent in the Philippine–American War (1899–1902), the only difference being that the unit designation inscribed on the scroll was replaced by the state's name.",
  OH: "The flag of Ohio, also known as the Ohio Burgee, is a red, white, and blue U.S. state flag with a distinctive triangular swallowtail shape. The flag's elements symbolize the state's natural features and order of admission into the Union. A prominent disc at the flag's hoist suggests the letter O, the first letter in Ohio's name.",
  OK: "The flag of the U.S. state of Oklahoma, also known as the Oklahoma flag, is a rectangular field of sky blue on which is placed an Osage war shield with six crosses and seven pendant eagle feathers above the name of the state in a white Eurostile text in all capitals. Superimposed onto the crosses of the war shield is a calumet and an olive branch.",
  OR: "The flag of Oregon is a two-sided flag in navy blue and gold with an optional gold fringe. On the front is the escutcheon from the state seal, and on the reverse is a gold figure of a beaver, the state animal. Oregon is the only U.S. state to feature different designs on either side of its flag.",
  PA: "The flag of the U.S. state of Pennsylvania consists of a blue field on which the state coat of arms is displayed.",
  RI: "The state flag of Rhode Island is the official flag of the U.S. state of Rhode Island, consisting of a white background and a gold anchor in the center surrounded by thirteen gold stars, for the original Thirteen Colonies and Rhode Island's status as the 13th state to ratify the Constitution. A blue ribbon below the anchor bears the state's motto in gold: 'HOPE'. The flag is frequently depicted with golden fringe around the edges of the flag, although the fringe is never actually on the flag unless it is used in federal-level displays, much like the national flag.",
  SC: "The flag of South Carolina is a symbol of the U.S. state of South Carolina consisting of a blue field with a white palmetto tree and a white crescent. Roots of this design have existed in some form since 1775, being based on one of the first American Revolutionary War flags. While keeping most of its design intact since its adoption, it has varied over the years.",
  SD: "The current flag of South Dakota was adopted in 1992 to represent the U.S. state of South Dakota. It consists of a field of sky blue charged with a version of the state seal in the center, surrounded by gold triangles representing the sun's rays, surrounded in turn by inscriptions in gold sans-serif capitals of 'south dakota' on top and 'the mount rushmore state' on the bottom. The sun represents the common weather in South Dakota.",
  TN: "The flag of Tennessee is the official flag of the U.S. state of Tennessee. The flag displays an emblem on a field of red, with a strip of blue bordered by white on the fly. The emblem in the middle consists of three white stars on a blue circle also with a white border. The central emblem portion of the flag has been adopted as the state's unofficial logo, and appears in the logos of some Tennessee-based companies and sports teams. Examples include the First Horizon Bank and the Tennessee Titans. It is quite common, however, to mess up the aforementioned emblem. The most common of these are placing the flag upside down.",
  TX: "The flag of Texas, also known as the Lone Star Flag, is the official flag of the U.S. state of Texas. Along with the flag of Hawaii, it is one of two state flags to have previously served as a national flag of an independent country.",
  UT: "The flag of the U.S. state of Utah was adopted on March 9, 2024, and is one of the official flags of the state. It is a horizontal tricolor of irregular blue, white, and red bands, with the white band featuring a gold-outlined blue hexagon containing a gold beehive above a five-pointed white star.",
  VT: "The flag of the U.S. state of Vermont displays the state's coat of arms and motto on a rectangular blue background. The Vermont General Assembly adopted the flag on June 1, 1923.",
  VA: "The seal of Virginia is the official symbolic representation of the Commonwealth of Virginia, a U.S. state. The state flag of Virginia consists of the obverse of the seal against a blue background. A state flag was first adopted at the beginning of the American Civil War in April 1861, readopted in 1912, and standardized by the General Assembly in February 1950. The standing allegorical female figure of virtue is shown having vanquished tyranny, symbolized by a fallen king at her feet. She has an exposed breast in the manner of classical depictions of Amazons. The motto shown on the seal, Sic semper tyrannis, means 'Thus always to tyrants.'",
  WA: "The flag of Washington consists of the state's seal, a portrait of George Washington, on a dark green field with optional gold fringe. It is the only U.S. state flag with a green field, as well as the only state flag with the likeness of a U.S. president. The secretary of state regulates flag protocol related to the state flag, as well as approving replica flags for commercial sale and other standards related to the flag.",
  WV: "The state flag of the U.S. state of West Virginia was officially adopted by the West Virginia Legislature on March 7, 1929. Its design consists of a pure white field bordered by a blue stripe with the coat of arms of West Virginia in the center, wreathed by Rhododendron maximum and topped by an unfurled red ribbon reading, 'State of West Virginia.' It is the only state flag to bear crossing rifles, meant to illustrate the importance of the state's fight for liberty during the American Civil War as the southern unionist 35th state.",
  WY: "The flag of the U.S. state of Wyoming was adopted on January 31, 1917. Its design consists of a blue field bordered by white and red, with a white silhouette of an American bison in the center, bearing the state seal rendered in blue on its side.",
  PR: "The flag of Puerto Rico, officially the Flag of the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, represents Puerto Rico and its people. It consists of five equal horizontal stripes, alternating from red to white, with a blue equilateral triangle based on the hoist side bearing a large, sharp, upright, five-pointed white star in the center. The white star stands for the archipelago and island, the three sides of the triangle for the three branches of the government, the blue for the sky and coastal waters, the red for the blood shed by warriors, and the white for liberty, victory, and peace. The flag is popularly known as the Monoestrellada, meaning having a lone star, or being single-starred. It is in the Stars and Stripes flag family.",
  GU: "The flag of Guam was adopted on February 9, 1948. The territorial flag is a dark blue field with a narrow red border on all sides. The red border – a later addition – represents the blood spilled during World War II and earlier Spanish sovereignty. In the center of the flag is the seal of Guam; almond in shape, it depicts a proa sailing in Hagåtña Bay near Hagåtña, and GUAM in red letters.",
  AS: "The flag of American Samoa consists of a red-edged white triangle pointing towards the hoist charged with a bald eagle clutching a war club and fly-whisk, with dark blue upper and lower triangles. It was adopted in April 1960 to replace the 'Stars and Stripes' as the official flag of the territory. The colors used epitomize the traditional colors of the United States and Samoa.",
  VI: "The flag of the United States Virgin Islands was adopted on May 17, 1921. It consists of a simplified version of the coat of arms of the United States between the letters V and I. The yellow-colored eagle holds a sprig of laurel in one talon, which symbolizes victory, and three blue arrows in the other, which represent the three major islands that make up the U.S. Virgin Islands: Saint Croix, Saint Thomas, and Saint John. When the U.S. Virgin Islands were owned by Denmark, the flag was a blue ensign with the Danish flag, or Dannebrog, in the canton.",
  MP: "The national flag of the United States commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands is a blue field, with a white five-pointed star superimposed on a gray latte stone encircled by a multicolored lei. It was originally adopted in 1972 without the lei. The current version of the flag was first flown July 4, 1976, with slight changes made to the flag in 1991 and 1995. The blue field represents the Pacific Ocean, the white star represents the commonwealth, and the latte stone and lei represent Chamorro and Carolinian culture, endemic to the island chain.",
};
